1
00:00:14,014 --> 00:00:18,018
You're watching <i>Chef & My Fridge</i>
with very special guests.

2
00:00:18,084 --> 00:00:21,321
Can you see
that we have a few more chairs today?

3
00:00:21,388 --> 00:00:23,757
-Let's welcome the special guests.
-Who could it be?

4
00:00:23,823 --> 00:00:26,259
Please welcome today's fridge owners.

5
00:00:26,326 --> 00:00:27,460
Come on out!

6
00:00:30,130 --> 00:00:32,465
Today's fridge owners.

7
00:00:34,034 --> 00:00:35,402
Looking cool!

8
00:00:36,736 --> 00:00:38,438
The sisters, BIBI and NaKyoung,

9
00:00:38,505 --> 00:00:40,874
and the siblings, Jonathan and Patricia.

10
00:00:40,940 --> 00:00:42,175
-Hello.
-Hello.

11
00:00:42,409 --> 00:00:43,443
WELCOME

12
00:00:43,510 --> 00:00:45,011
Welcome.

13
00:00:45,078 --> 00:00:46,413
-Hello.
-This way.

14
00:00:46,479 --> 00:00:47,680
Hold my hand.

15
00:00:47,747 --> 00:00:49,249
LEAVES

16
00:00:49,315 --> 00:00:51,718
-You have to hold my hand.
-I have to?

17
00:00:51,785 --> 00:00:53,820
-You should hold her hand.
-Check on your sister.

18
00:00:53,887 --> 00:00:55,155
-Goodness.
-No way.

19
00:00:55,455 --> 00:00:57,257
SQUEAL

20
00:00:57,323 --> 00:00:58,958
We've put together…

21
00:00:59,025 --> 00:01:00,960
-How special.
-…a siblings special.

22
00:01:01,027 --> 00:01:02,729
Allow me to introduce them.

23
00:01:02,796 --> 00:01:06,399
Please welcome BIBI
and NaKyoung of tripleS.

24
00:01:06,466 --> 00:01:07,400
Welcome.

25
00:01:08,201 --> 00:01:10,070
WELCOMING WILDLY

26
00:01:10,136 --> 00:01:11,471
Hello.

27
00:01:11,538 --> 00:01:12,806
I am… Um…

28
00:01:13,606 --> 00:01:15,241
I sing and act,

29
00:01:15,308 --> 00:01:16,342
and I'm Kim Hyeong-seo…

30
00:01:16,409 --> 00:01:17,811
I mean, BIBI.

31
00:01:17,877 --> 00:01:19,245
She must be really nervous.

32
00:01:19,312 --> 00:01:21,514
You're Kim Hyeong-seo when you act,
and BIBI when you sing.

33
00:01:21,581 --> 00:01:22,949
BIBI Kim Hyeong-seo.

34
00:01:23,016 --> 00:01:25,118
Hello, I'm a member of tripleS,

35
00:01:25,185 --> 00:01:27,554
and the most charming one of the 24,

36
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:29,422
NaKyoung.

37
00:01:29,489 --> 00:01:31,224
Yes, she's right.

38
00:01:31,291 --> 00:01:33,059
Among 24 members.

39
00:01:33,126 --> 00:01:35,261
-Let's go over to that side.
-Yes, these siblings.

40
00:01:35,328 --> 00:01:37,063
<i>Spicy, oh so spicy</i>

41
00:01:37,130 --> 00:01:38,465
-Their diss battle…
-Sorry?

42
00:01:38,531 --> 00:01:41,734
-"Spicy."
-It's different for us.

43
00:01:41,801 --> 00:01:44,871
The comical siblings' diss battle
took over the TV show industry.

44
00:01:44,938 --> 00:01:48,208
Please welcome Jonathan and Patricia.
Welcome.

45
00:01:48,274 --> 00:01:50,477
-All right.
-Woo-hoo!

46
00:01:51,010 --> 00:01:52,979
-Hi…
-Hello, all the way from Jeolla-do,

47
00:01:53,046 --> 00:01:55,248
-…I'm Jonathan.
-And I'm Patricia.

48
00:01:55,315 --> 00:01:56,216
Hello.

49
00:01:57,484 --> 00:02:00,053
HOW ADORABLE

50
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:02,021
All the way from Jeolla-do?

51
00:02:02,088 --> 00:02:04,290
HOMEY DIALECT

52
00:02:04,357 --> 00:02:06,693
Where are you two from?

53
00:02:06,759 --> 00:02:09,095
We were born
in the Democratic Republic of the Congo.

54
00:02:09,162 --> 00:02:10,964
I moved to Korea when I was eight,

55
00:02:11,030 --> 00:02:12,932
then went to Jeolla-do in "sigth" grade.

56
00:02:12,999 --> 00:02:15,034
-"Sigth."
-"Sigth."

57
00:02:15,101 --> 00:02:17,537
THE ORIGINAL JEOLLA-DO DIALECT

58
00:02:17,604 --> 00:02:20,073
-So we're from Gwangju.
-Gwangju.

59
00:02:20,140 --> 00:02:22,642
BIBI and NaKyoung, where are you two from?

60
00:02:22,709 --> 00:02:25,278
Born in Busan, raised in Ulsan,

61
00:02:25,345 --> 00:02:27,614
then went to school in Changwon.

62
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:29,549
We pretty much kept to Gyeongsang-do.

63
00:02:29,616 --> 00:02:31,784
This is our Hwagae Market special.

64
00:02:31,851 --> 00:02:33,786
We're in for a lot of fun today.

65
00:02:33,853 --> 00:02:36,656
BIBI claimed
to have grown up with <i>Chef & My Fridge.</i>

66
00:02:36,723 --> 00:02:39,292
So you've been a fan since you were 15?

67
00:02:39,359 --> 00:02:41,027
I've watched it since the first episode.

68
00:02:41,094 --> 00:02:42,595
-Really?
-Back in 2014?

69
00:02:42,662 --> 00:02:44,497
Yes, when you launched the show.

70
00:02:44,564 --> 00:02:45,865
-Are you serious?
-Yes.

71
00:02:45,932 --> 00:02:47,267
Internet TV was a thing,

72
00:02:47,333 --> 00:02:49,936
so I'd buy the show

73
00:02:50,003 --> 00:02:52,939
-for 1,190 won per episode.
-You remember that?

74
00:02:53,006 --> 00:02:55,208
-You paid for the show? 1,190 won?
-I did.

75
00:02:55,275 --> 00:02:57,911
-Yes.
-She did that? She's a real fan.

76
00:02:57,977 --> 00:03:01,114
-I really loved it.
-She watched it so often.

77
00:03:01,181 --> 00:03:03,783
That's why I'm so nervous right now.

78
00:03:03,850 --> 00:03:08,755
We took a few years off
before returning, right?

79
00:03:08,821 --> 00:03:12,692
-By then, I was already someone on TV.
-Yes, you had debuted.

80
00:03:12,759 --> 00:03:14,460
I told myself, "I'll be on the show."

81
00:03:14,527 --> 00:03:16,262
"I'll be on the show!"

82
00:03:16,563 --> 00:03:17,497
HER BUCKET LIST

83
00:03:17,564 --> 00:03:18,998
You'll somehow be on the show.

84
00:03:19,065 --> 00:03:20,600
"I'll somehow get myself on it."

85
00:03:20,667 --> 00:03:21,701
And I'm here!

86
00:03:22,402 --> 00:03:23,236
SQUEAL

87
00:03:23,303 --> 00:03:24,737
She's on the show.

88
00:03:24,804 --> 00:03:27,307
She grew up with the show,
and now she's on it.

89
00:03:27,373 --> 00:03:29,776
Did you watch the show with BIBI?

90
00:03:29,842 --> 00:03:30,777
I did.

91
00:03:30,843 --> 00:03:34,547
She always had dibs
on the remote control, so…

92
00:03:34,847 --> 00:03:36,683
THE REMOTE CONTROL BELONGS TO THE ELDEST

93
00:03:36,749 --> 00:03:39,886
The hierarchy seems
quite established with that family.

94
00:03:39,953 --> 00:03:41,788
What about you?

95
00:03:41,854 --> 00:03:45,258
We had to work for it. For example…

96
00:03:45,325 --> 00:03:47,260
When our teachers ended the day

97
00:03:47,327 --> 00:03:49,862
with the homeroom meeting,

98
00:03:49,929 --> 00:03:52,865
I'd sit like this
and shoot out as soon as we were done.

99
00:03:52,932 --> 00:03:54,867
DASHING BACK HOME

100
00:03:54,934 --> 00:03:57,103
Whoever gets the remote control is it.

101
00:03:57,170 --> 00:03:58,338
Whoever gets it.

102
00:03:58,404 --> 00:04:01,107
-Grab it first to win.
-Grab it first to win.

103
00:04:01,174 --> 00:04:03,309
So you take it with you
even to the bathroom.

104
00:04:03,376 --> 00:04:05,712
NEVER LET YOUR GUARD DOWN

105
00:04:05,778 --> 00:04:07,280
It doesn't matter where I go.

106
00:04:07,347 --> 00:04:10,149
I have to take it with me
no matter where I go.

107
00:04:10,216 --> 00:04:11,451
AMAZED…

108
00:04:11,517 --> 00:04:12,986
But you never took it by force.

109
00:04:13,052 --> 00:04:15,488
-We never did that.
-We didn't do that.

110
00:04:15,555 --> 00:04:18,358
We heard from a tipster that Patricia

111
00:04:18,424 --> 00:04:20,994
has been envious of NaKyoung.

112
00:04:21,060 --> 00:04:22,495
You're right.

113
00:04:22,562 --> 00:04:25,398
I've always wanted
an older sister since I was young.

114
00:04:25,465 --> 00:04:27,467
I also saw them on <i>Men on a Mission,</i>

115
00:04:27,533 --> 00:04:31,037
and the way BIBI looked at her

116
00:04:31,104 --> 00:04:32,605
seemed so loving, so I was jealous.

117
00:04:32,672 --> 00:04:33,706
That's far from the truth.

118
00:04:33,973 --> 00:04:36,109
THE LOOK THAT PATRICIA SAW

119
00:04:36,175 --> 00:04:38,778
The look in her eyes
was definitely different.

120
00:04:38,845 --> 00:04:42,315
Also, there are so many things
that sisters can share with one another,

121
00:04:42,382 --> 00:04:45,618
-such as clothes and makeup.
-That's true.

122
00:04:45,685 --> 00:04:47,020
So what's it like, NaKyoung?

123
00:04:47,086 --> 00:04:51,090
-I can always borrow her clothes.
-Right? And makeup.

124
00:04:51,157 --> 00:04:53,693
And there are things
that sisters can talk about.

125
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:54,694
It can be fun for you.

126
00:04:54,761 --> 00:04:56,329
Do you want to try it for a month?

127
00:04:57,497 --> 00:04:58,765
You're handing her over?

128
00:04:58,831 --> 00:05:02,268
It's like you want to get rid of me.
"Do you want to try it for a month?"

129
00:05:02,702 --> 00:05:04,604
EVEN JEALOUS OF THEM BICKERING♥

130
00:05:04,671 --> 00:05:08,641
I'm sure BIBI has made you feel
wronged and frustrated before.

131
00:05:08,708 --> 00:05:12,345
I've been a victim
of her gaslighting quite a lot,

132
00:05:12,412 --> 00:05:13,913
-especially with food.
-Food?

133
00:05:13,980 --> 00:05:15,348
-Me too.
-Especially so

134
00:05:15,415 --> 00:05:18,518
because she's really into food.

135
00:05:18,584 --> 00:05:20,820
I'm a bit of a glutton.

136
00:05:20,887 --> 00:05:23,890
-There was a candy called <i>Jjakggung.</i>
-I know that.

137
00:05:23,956 --> 00:05:25,358
GRAPE & STRAWBERRY FLAVORED

138
00:05:25,425 --> 00:05:27,393
Everyone knows that

139
00:05:27,460 --> 00:05:29,896
-the grape flavor is much better.
-It's absolutely better.

140
00:05:29,962 --> 00:05:30,997
But she'd say,

141
00:05:31,064 --> 00:05:32,765
"Strawberry flavor is much better."

142
00:05:32,832 --> 00:05:35,935
I'd go, "Look, it's pink."

143
00:05:36,002 --> 00:05:37,470
"It's such a pretty pink."

144
00:05:37,537 --> 00:05:38,705
Why do I feel so guilty?

145
00:05:38,771 --> 00:05:39,872
(HAS A SISTER)

146
00:05:39,939 --> 00:05:40,840
So one day…

147
00:05:40,907 --> 00:05:41,908
HER GASLIGHTING DAYS…

148
00:05:41,974 --> 00:05:43,443
She'd usually eat it, though annoyed,

149
00:05:43,509 --> 00:05:45,745
but that day, she said,
"You eat it all, pig!"

150
00:05:47,180 --> 00:05:49,782
-She blew up. She threw it out there.
-She has had enough.

151
00:05:49,849 --> 00:05:52,418
She's not going to put up with it anymore.

152
00:05:52,485 --> 00:05:53,986
-Can't do that with food.
-It's…

153
00:05:54,053 --> 00:05:55,188
She won't take it every time.

154
00:05:55,455 --> 00:05:57,790
STILL, I WISH I HAD A SISTER♥

155
00:05:57,857 --> 00:05:59,659
Here's something Jonathan said.

156
00:05:59,726 --> 00:06:03,563
"Even if Patricia had an older sister,
she would've been beaten a lot."

157
00:06:03,629 --> 00:06:04,530
That's right.

158
00:06:04,597 --> 00:06:06,799
-That's too harsh.
-Why did you say that?

159
00:06:06,866 --> 00:06:10,403
-She's the type who asks for trouble.
-She asks for trouble?

160
00:06:10,470 --> 00:06:11,437
No way.

161
00:06:11,504 --> 00:06:13,106
When she's not happy with something.

162
00:06:13,172 --> 00:06:15,641
-she immediately stops calling me <i>oppa.</i>
-That's not it.

163
00:06:15,708 --> 00:06:18,945
-She goes, "Hey," or "Jonathan."
-Wait, no.

164
00:06:19,011 --> 00:06:19,846
Do you do that?

165
00:06:19,912 --> 00:06:22,415
-I wouldn't call her "hey."
-She wouldn't.

166
00:06:22,482 --> 00:06:23,683
I don't say that.

167
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:25,218
Then what, in English?

168
00:06:25,284 --> 00:06:27,887
-I'd go, "Nathan."
-"Nathan."

169
00:06:27,954 --> 00:06:30,590
I say it all cute. "Nathan."

170
00:06:30,656 --> 00:06:31,891
Do you think she asks for trouble?

171
00:06:31,958 --> 00:06:34,494
Very often.

172
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:36,262
When I was young,

173
00:06:36,329 --> 00:06:38,464
-he once punched me with his fist.
-My gosh.

174
00:06:38,531 --> 00:06:40,366
You'll see that my face is asymmetrical.

175
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:43,336
A SPICY EXPOSÉ

176
00:06:43,703 --> 00:06:45,538
CHUCKLING…

177
00:06:45,605 --> 00:06:47,707
My left side
looks different from the right.

178
00:06:47,774 --> 00:06:49,409
-You.
-I can see it.

179
00:06:49,475 --> 00:06:51,811
-You're dying to get TV gigs.
-He punched me like this.

180
00:06:51,878 --> 00:06:53,846
-Like this.
-Wait a second.

181
00:06:53,913 --> 00:06:55,748
Jonathan, let us hear her out first.

182
00:06:55,815 --> 00:06:59,085
-He has always loved milk tea.
-Milk tea.

183
00:06:59,152 --> 00:07:00,720
HEADACHE

184
00:07:00,787 --> 00:07:02,889
One day, he made a pretty big batch,

185
00:07:02,955 --> 00:07:06,726
so I asked him for a sip,
but he wouldn't give it to me.

186
00:07:06,793 --> 00:07:08,995
I was young and quite mad,

187
00:07:09,061 --> 00:07:10,329
so I went behind his back

188
00:07:10,396 --> 00:07:11,431
and spit into it.

189
00:07:11,664 --> 00:07:12,565
WHAT?

190
00:07:12,632 --> 00:07:14,200
-Into his tea?
-You spit into it?

191
00:07:14,267 --> 00:07:16,068
I spit into it,

192
00:07:16,135 --> 00:07:19,038
and I guess he must've seen me do it.

193
00:07:19,105 --> 00:07:20,239
NODDING

194
00:07:20,306 --> 00:07:22,842
"You're going to get punched, you know."
When he said that, I went…

195
00:07:23,209 --> 00:07:24,343
BLEHHHHH

196
00:07:24,844 --> 00:07:26,612
TEASING WITH ALL HER MIGHT

197
00:07:26,679 --> 00:07:29,749
-That was all I could do.
-That's annoying.

198
00:07:29,816 --> 00:07:32,819
He warned me three times,

199
00:07:32,885 --> 00:07:35,087
then I blacked out.

200
00:07:35,154 --> 00:07:36,789
He had punched me.

201
00:07:37,423 --> 00:07:40,092
WHOOSH…

202
00:07:40,159 --> 00:07:41,928
I had a huge pride, so I said,

203
00:07:41,994 --> 00:07:43,529
"It didn't even hurt."

204
00:07:43,596 --> 00:07:45,631
Then I cried in exactly three seconds.

205
00:07:45,698 --> 00:07:48,568
"It didn't even hurt…" Then I bawled.

206
00:07:48,634 --> 00:07:50,203
When she said it didn't hurt,

207
00:07:50,269 --> 00:07:52,438
I was getting ready to punch her again.

208
00:07:52,505 --> 00:07:55,041
She said it didn't hurt,
so I was getting myself ready.

209
00:07:55,107 --> 00:07:57,176
I was like this,
but thankfully, she cried.

210
00:07:57,243 --> 00:07:59,479
WHAT A RELIEF

211
00:07:59,545 --> 00:08:02,114
-I shouldn't have done that. Drink this.
-"Drink this."

212
00:08:02,181 --> 00:08:03,716
-Thanks.
-They're funny.

213
00:08:03,783 --> 00:08:05,751
-Thanks.
-He opened it for you.

214
00:08:05,818 --> 00:08:08,254
What's this about you two wrestling often?

215
00:08:08,321 --> 00:08:11,591
Patricia claims that
she's been the only one who got abused,

216
00:08:11,657 --> 00:08:13,793
but it went both ways.

217
00:08:13,860 --> 00:08:16,095
There's something called WWE,

218
00:08:16,162 --> 00:08:17,430
and I'm a huge fan.

219
00:08:17,697 --> 00:08:19,065
I CAN SEE WHAT'S COMING…

220
00:08:19,131 --> 00:08:20,967
-So I'd lay out a futon…
-Right.

221
00:08:21,033 --> 00:08:22,568
With his younger sister?

222
00:08:22,635 --> 00:08:24,237
-…and wrestle with her.
-Really?

223
00:08:24,303 --> 00:08:25,304
And it was a fair fight?

224
00:08:25,371 --> 00:08:26,539
-So fair.
-Totally.

225
00:08:26,606 --> 00:08:27,907
We were on par with each other.

226
00:08:27,974 --> 00:08:29,242
-An equal fight.
-Yes.

227
00:08:29,308 --> 00:08:31,844
-We were in elementary school.
-Back in elementary school.

228
00:08:31,911 --> 00:08:33,913
-In elementary school.
-It was an equal fight?

229
00:08:33,980 --> 00:08:36,182
I'd be lying there,
telling Patricia to jump from the sofa.

230
00:08:36,249 --> 00:08:38,084
-Slam!
-Just like that.

231
00:08:38,150 --> 00:08:40,620
At times, she'd do this with her elbow.

232
00:08:40,686 --> 00:08:44,690
We put out the futon and did that
at least three times a week.

233
00:08:44,757 --> 00:08:45,758
But it wasn't a real fight.

234
00:08:45,825 --> 00:08:47,894
-It wasn't a real fight.
-No.

235
00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:51,464
Just like them,
our fight also wasn't one-sided.

236
00:08:51,531 --> 00:08:53,299
It was a one-on-one fight.

237
00:08:53,366 --> 00:08:54,834
-It was a one-on-one fight?
-It was.

238
00:08:54,901 --> 00:08:56,903
Out of ten fights,
she'd win at least four.

239
00:08:56,969 --> 00:08:58,804
THEY'RE NECK-AND-NECK TOO

240
00:08:58,871 --> 00:09:00,306
She has that grit.

241
00:09:00,573 --> 00:09:02,174
GOT GRIT

242
00:09:02,241 --> 00:09:03,709
-How do sisters fight?
-Was she good?

243
00:09:04,176 --> 00:09:05,011
GRABS

244
00:09:05,478 --> 00:09:07,046
SQUEAL

245
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:08,614
A TUG-OF-WAR?

246
00:09:08,681 --> 00:09:09,916
Do you pull out each other's hair?

247
00:09:09,982 --> 00:09:11,651
-We have rules too.
-You had rules?

248
00:09:11,717 --> 00:09:13,352
It's something like this.

249
00:09:13,686 --> 00:09:15,221
BUILDING TENSION

250
00:09:15,288 --> 00:09:17,290
So whoever is pushed back loses?

251
00:09:17,356 --> 00:09:18,224
Something like that.

252
00:09:18,291 --> 00:09:20,526
It's just like
how a wrestling match starts.

253
00:09:20,593 --> 00:09:22,328
-It's like that.
-It's similar.

254
00:09:23,129 --> 00:09:24,297
It's similar.

255
00:09:24,897 --> 00:09:28,634
Eun-yeong, you have a twin sister, right?

256
00:09:28,701 --> 00:09:31,237
We were the double high beams
of Geumjeong-dong.

257
00:09:31,304 --> 00:09:33,806
-We were famous.
-The dazzling sisters.

258
00:09:33,873 --> 00:09:36,309
I think our fights were pretty intense.

259
00:09:36,375 --> 00:09:39,078
We often made use of objects. For example,

260
00:09:39,145 --> 00:09:41,280
-we'd throw a fan at each other.
-A fan?

261
00:09:41,347 --> 00:09:43,282
-A fan?
-When we did that,

262
00:09:43,349 --> 00:09:46,519
-…the neck of the fan got bent.
-Right.

263
00:09:46,586 --> 00:09:48,854
Our parents told us
to keep using the same fan

264
00:09:48,921 --> 00:09:50,823
because we did that in a fight,

265
00:09:50,890 --> 00:09:54,961
so we literally used this broken
and shaky fan for five years.

266
00:09:55,027 --> 00:09:55,861
What are you saying?

267
00:09:55,928 --> 00:09:57,763
-Shaky?
-Yes, also,

268
00:09:57,830 --> 00:10:01,067
-…do you remember D's juice bottle?
-Yes, that one is really sturdy.

269
00:10:01,133 --> 00:10:03,369
I once threw that at my sister.

270
00:10:03,436 --> 00:10:05,972
-But that woman dodged it right away.
-"That woman"?

271
00:10:06,038 --> 00:10:08,040
-"That woman"?
-"That woman"?

272
00:10:08,107 --> 00:10:09,542
It's like a scene from <i>Rustic Period.</i>

273
00:10:09,609 --> 00:10:11,711
-She dodged it.
-She went for her sister.

274
00:10:12,011 --> 00:10:13,045
BRUTAL

275
00:10:13,112 --> 00:10:15,214
So our fights were pretty intense.

276
00:10:15,281 --> 00:10:16,616
See? I'm much better.

277
00:10:16,682 --> 00:10:18,317
NOT TRUE

278
00:10:18,684 --> 00:10:20,753
ONLY THE STRONGEST SURVIVED
IN GEUMJEONG-DONG

279
00:10:20,820 --> 00:10:22,488
You may fight from time to time,

280
00:10:22,555 --> 00:10:24,790
-but you probably make up with each other.
-That's important.

281
00:10:24,857 --> 00:10:26,058
In our case,

282
00:10:26,125 --> 00:10:28,027
I always initiate it.

283
00:10:28,094 --> 00:10:30,429
-You request it first?
-I knew it. It's the older sister.

284
00:10:30,496 --> 00:10:32,798
"I'm getting <i>tteokbokki.</i>
Are you going to eat some?"

285
00:10:32,865 --> 00:10:34,767
I know what that is. That's so accurate.

286
00:10:35,568 --> 00:10:36,636
I KNOW WHAT THAT IS

287
00:10:36,702 --> 00:10:37,737
I know that vibe.

288
00:10:38,337 --> 00:10:39,939
"I'm making <i>ramyeon. </i>Do you want some?"

289
00:10:40,239 --> 00:10:41,974
THE SISTERS' CUTE FORMULA TO MAKE PEACE

290
00:10:42,041 --> 00:10:43,909
-That's cute.
-One time at an awards ceremony,

291
00:10:43,976 --> 00:10:46,312
NaKyoung did a kissing performance

292
00:10:46,379 --> 00:10:50,516
-out in the open for everyone to see.
-You kissed her?

293
00:10:50,583 --> 00:10:53,619
-We were both doing well in that season,
-Why?

294
00:10:53,686 --> 00:10:56,022
…so we saw each other
all the time at ceremonies.

295
00:10:56,088 --> 00:10:57,456
-I see.
-One day,

296
00:10:57,523 --> 00:11:00,092
we ran into each other at the venue,

297
00:11:00,159 --> 00:11:02,795
and she said,
"I'll kiss you if you win an award today."

298
00:11:02,995 --> 00:11:04,263
OH MY

299
00:11:04,830 --> 00:11:06,732
I thought, "Sure, great for me."

300
00:11:06,799 --> 00:11:08,567
Looking back, I think
she took advantage of me.

301
00:11:08,634 --> 00:11:10,002
How did she do that?

302
00:11:10,069 --> 00:11:11,404
It went viral immediately.

303
00:11:11,470 --> 00:11:12,905
-She had a plan.
-Just like Sung-jun.

304
00:11:13,706 --> 00:11:14,774
She's good.

305
00:11:14,840 --> 00:11:17,076
Honestly, she may have
taken advantage of me,

306
00:11:17,143 --> 00:11:18,377
but I was still proud.

307
00:11:18,944 --> 00:11:22,214
"She has become someone
who knows how to get things her way."

308
00:11:22,281 --> 00:11:23,849
"My sister is a big girl now."

309
00:11:23,916 --> 00:11:25,051
She is.

310
00:11:25,117 --> 00:11:26,986
What were you really thinking?

311
00:11:27,053 --> 00:11:28,621
-She's precisely right.
-Precisely right?

312
00:11:28,921 --> 00:11:30,356
SHE MADE PRECISE USE OF HER SISTER

313
00:11:30,423 --> 00:11:32,258
-Precisely right? She's good.
-It's like this.

314
00:11:32,324 --> 00:11:34,894
I had an ambitious plan.

315
00:11:34,960 --> 00:11:38,030
Also, she likes it when I kiss her.

316
00:11:38,097 --> 00:11:38,964
She likes it.

317
00:11:39,031 --> 00:11:42,768
Our family tends to be
very physically affectionate.

318
00:11:42,835 --> 00:11:45,571
Light kisses happen all the time.

319
00:11:45,938 --> 00:11:47,606
UNIMAGINABLE…

320
00:11:47,673 --> 00:11:49,141
Kisses on the cheek happen daily.

321
00:11:49,208 --> 00:11:51,610
-I can do it now.
-You can?

322
00:11:52,411 --> 00:11:53,913
WHOA!

323
00:11:54,513 --> 00:11:55,648
QUITE SHOCKED

324
00:11:56,048 --> 00:11:57,516
THE SIBLINGS HAVE NEVER SEEN THIS HAPPEN

325
00:11:57,583 --> 00:11:59,251
At an awards ceremony,

326
00:11:59,318 --> 00:12:01,587
he practically pushed me over.

327
00:12:01,654 --> 00:12:04,090
I fell at an awards show.

328
00:12:04,156 --> 00:12:05,725
Now that I heard that story,

329
00:12:05,791 --> 00:12:09,261
I realize they were kissing there
while he was knocking me over.

330
00:12:09,328 --> 00:12:11,063
He got embarrassed about me.

331
00:12:11,130 --> 00:12:13,032
Can you two do it too?

332
00:12:13,099 --> 00:12:15,401
That's the worst. I can't do that to him.

333
00:12:15,701 --> 00:12:17,002
SHE'S ABOUT TO GET FURIOUS

334
00:12:17,069 --> 00:12:18,270
But you're family.

335
00:12:18,337 --> 00:12:20,406
-Because we're family.
-Because you're family?

336
00:12:20,473 --> 00:12:22,274
That doesn't happen between siblings.

337
00:12:22,341 --> 00:12:23,576
How loving can you be?

338
00:12:23,642 --> 00:12:25,478
-Sitting next to each other.
-Yes.

339
00:12:25,544 --> 00:12:27,913
Sharing the same air in the same space.

340
00:12:28,314 --> 00:12:29,849
THE MOST THEY CAN DO IS SHARE AIR

341
00:12:29,915 --> 00:12:33,385
Or how you end up
rubbing on each other when you fight.

342
00:12:33,452 --> 00:12:35,921
-Rubbing skin.
-Yes.

343
00:12:35,988 --> 00:12:38,090
-I'd throw her, and when she returns,
-Yes, throwing.

344
00:12:38,157 --> 00:12:39,291
-…I'd pin her down.
-That's it.

345
00:12:39,358 --> 00:12:40,960
-Wrestling.
-That's all.

346
00:12:41,327 --> 00:12:43,062
THE SIBLINGS' TAKE: WRESTLING IS LOVING

347
00:12:43,129 --> 00:12:45,664
Right now, we're the most physical
we have ever been.

348
00:12:45,731 --> 00:12:47,900
-This doesn't happen often.
-I see.

349
00:12:47,967 --> 00:12:50,369
-Sitting next to each other?
-We're dead serious.

350
00:12:50,436 --> 00:12:52,271
Just sharing the same air.

351
00:12:52,338 --> 00:12:54,473
What if you go really viral
for kissing his cheek?

352
00:12:54,540 --> 00:12:57,443
-No, thanks.
-It'll literally give us viruses.

353
00:12:57,510 --> 00:12:58,477
THE VIRAL SIBLINGS

354
00:12:58,544 --> 00:12:59,678
Getting sick.

355
00:12:59,745 --> 00:13:02,948
We once had to hold hands
while shooting an ad.

356
00:13:03,015 --> 00:13:04,483
It must've been so hard.

357
00:13:04,550 --> 00:13:06,385
-We had to hold it for a while.
-Right.

358
00:13:06,452 --> 00:13:08,187
-They kept insisting.
-You'd do it for money?

359
00:13:08,254 --> 00:13:10,189
-Yes.
-We can do that.

360
00:13:10,923 --> 00:13:12,158
They'd do it for money.

361
00:13:12,224 --> 00:13:14,326
-They'll need cash.
-Yes, right.

362
00:13:14,393 --> 00:13:16,061
It's in the contract.

363
00:13:16,128 --> 00:13:17,797
Hug, plus so-and-so won.

364
00:13:17,863 --> 00:13:20,266
EXTRA CHARGES FOR PHYSICAL TOUCH:
HOLD HANDS, HUG, KISS

365
00:13:22,268 --> 00:13:24,170
The rate also goes by the minute.

366
00:13:24,236 --> 00:13:26,105
-So the rate changes?
-It does.

367
00:13:26,172 --> 00:13:27,006
STRATEGIC WITH NUMBERS

368
00:13:27,072 --> 00:13:28,707
-So you two can touch for money.
-Yes.

369
00:13:28,774 --> 00:13:31,343
-We're business-oriented.
-Exclusively business-oriented.

370
00:13:31,410 --> 00:13:33,712
-They're true siblings.
-They are.

371
00:13:33,779 --> 00:13:36,015
We're now about to go through

372
00:13:36,081 --> 00:13:38,851
the Gyeongsang-do sisters,
BIBI and NaKyoung's fridge.

373
00:13:38,918 --> 00:13:42,121
Do you two use this together?

374
00:13:42,188 --> 00:13:44,456
The fridge itself is mine.

375
00:13:44,523 --> 00:13:47,459
But we often make something
whenever she comes over.

376
00:13:47,526 --> 00:13:50,196
Yes, I go over often,

377
00:13:50,262 --> 00:13:53,199
-so you'll see some traces of mine.
-I see.

378
00:13:53,265 --> 00:13:54,934
I starved myself yesterday.

379
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:56,969
-Because we took your fridge.
-Because of that.

380
00:13:57,036 --> 00:13:58,537
Right. We take everything.

381
00:13:58,604 --> 00:14:01,974
-We present BIBI and NaKyoung's fridge!
-Fridge!

382
00:14:03,209 --> 00:14:04,910
THEY HAVE LOTS OF STUFF!

383
00:14:05,511 --> 00:14:08,447
PRESENTING BIBI & KIM NAKYOUNG'S FRIDGE

384
00:14:08,948 --> 00:14:09,782
THE FRIDGE IS LOADED

385
00:14:09,849 --> 00:14:11,283
Your fridge is full.

386
00:14:11,350 --> 00:14:12,351
-Whoa.
-What?

387
00:14:12,418 --> 00:14:13,752
You must feed yourself well.

388
00:14:14,086 --> 00:14:15,020
SAUCE BINS ARE FULL TOO

389
00:14:15,087 --> 00:14:15,955
She has so many sauces.

390
00:14:16,021 --> 00:14:17,790
You do have a thing for food.

391
00:14:17,857 --> 00:14:19,425
-I'm quite obsessed.
-She does.

392
00:14:19,491 --> 00:14:20,993
-Wow.
-Whoa.

393
00:14:21,060 --> 00:14:23,262
-There are so many things.
-She has lots of stuff.

394
00:14:23,329 --> 00:14:25,764
-It's completely different from ours.
-Let's go through it.

395
00:14:25,831 --> 00:14:27,132
The top shelf.

396
00:14:27,199 --> 00:14:28,601
I see.

397
00:14:28,667 --> 00:14:31,670
-I see. The top shelf.
-What is it?

398
00:14:31,737 --> 00:14:33,939
This kimchi is completely dried up.

399
00:14:34,006 --> 00:14:34,974
You left it there?

400
00:14:35,040 --> 00:14:37,176
-Wait.
-It hasn't been long since she had it.

401
00:14:37,576 --> 00:14:39,044
HARDENED ON THE PLATE

402
00:14:39,111 --> 00:14:41,347
-It has hardened.
-It has.

403
00:14:41,413 --> 00:14:43,082
This one is sitting out here too.

404
00:14:43,148 --> 00:14:46,085
This is a lot,
but do you keep it out in the open?

405
00:14:46,151 --> 00:14:48,554
Yes, and when I crave some,
I flip it over and eat it.

406
00:14:49,121 --> 00:14:50,589
Yes, that makes it new.

407
00:14:50,656 --> 00:14:53,225
-Flip it like this.
-Flip it like this, and it's new.

408
00:14:53,292 --> 00:14:55,828
-She's right.
-It's as good as new.

409
00:14:55,895 --> 00:14:57,596
You've watched us since the first episode,

410
00:14:57,663 --> 00:14:59,531
so you must know that
we show everything as is.

411
00:14:59,598 --> 00:15:00,933
But here's the thing.

412
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,836
It was quite realistic for a few episodes

413
00:15:03,903 --> 00:15:05,237
after the first one.

414
00:15:05,304 --> 00:15:06,872
Then I began to see good ingredients

415
00:15:06,939 --> 00:15:07,873
and clean fridges.

416
00:15:07,940 --> 00:15:09,642
-Yes.
-It didn't feel used.

417
00:15:09,708 --> 00:15:12,645
So I swore to show my real fridge
if I ever got to be on the show.

418
00:15:12,878 --> 00:15:13,879
THAT'S THE SPIRIT…

419
00:15:13,946 --> 00:15:15,948
That was what I had on my mind.

420
00:15:16,015 --> 00:15:17,383
What's here?

421
00:15:17,449 --> 00:15:19,518
-She has some peppers.
-Peppers.

422
00:15:19,585 --> 00:15:20,986
Do you like spicy food?

423
00:15:21,053 --> 00:15:23,255
-Aren't these really hot?
-Are those Thai chilies?

424
00:15:23,322 --> 00:15:25,491
-Vietnamese chilies.
-Yes, bird's eye chilies.

425
00:15:25,557 --> 00:15:28,093
We love spicy food so much

426
00:15:28,160 --> 00:15:30,162
and eat it
until it probably ruins our stomachs.

427
00:15:30,229 --> 00:15:31,730
-Whoa.
-Spicy food, I mean.

428
00:15:31,797 --> 00:15:33,599
-That's like us.
-Those who love spicy food…

429
00:15:33,666 --> 00:15:37,469
-…add Cheongyang pepper to everything.
-Yes.

430
00:15:37,536 --> 00:15:39,305
-We always do that.
-Always.

431
00:15:39,371 --> 00:15:41,373
-We need them before we have a bite.
-A spoon, a pepper.

432
00:15:42,274 --> 00:15:44,310
It gets your appetite going.

433
00:15:44,376 --> 00:15:45,644
HOW ADORABLE

434
00:15:45,711 --> 00:15:46,879
Do you two like spicy food?

435
00:15:46,946 --> 00:15:49,348
-Yes.
-So much. We love it.

436
00:15:49,415 --> 00:15:51,283
In Africa,

437
00:15:51,350 --> 00:15:53,319
there are these circular chilies,

438
00:15:53,385 --> 00:15:54,987
and they're insanely spicy.

439
00:15:55,054 --> 00:15:56,388
Really spicy.

440
00:15:56,455 --> 00:15:57,756
-Habanero.
-Habanero.

441
00:15:57,823 --> 00:15:59,124
Are Cheongyang peppers like a snack?

442
00:15:59,191 --> 00:16:01,360
FLEXING SPICE TOLERANCE

443
00:16:01,427 --> 00:16:03,028
When I feel like having a snack.

444
00:16:03,095 --> 00:16:04,263
It's like candy.

445
00:16:04,730 --> 00:16:05,564
FLEXING ON ANOTHER LEVEL

446
00:16:05,631 --> 00:16:07,866
-Like candy?
-It's that mild for you?

447
00:16:07,933 --> 00:16:09,301
It's like chewing gum.

448
00:16:09,368 --> 00:16:11,937
-Like candy.
-In that case,

449
00:16:12,004 --> 00:16:13,238
let's do a test.

450
00:16:13,305 --> 00:16:15,607
Let's see who's better with spicy food,

451
00:16:15,674 --> 00:16:17,543
the sisters or the siblings.

452
00:16:17,609 --> 00:16:18,911
You're eating that raw?

453
00:16:18,978 --> 00:16:20,379
I'll give you one each.

454
00:16:20,446 --> 00:16:22,648
-No<i>ssamjang?</i>
-I'm not as good anymore.

455
00:16:22,915 --> 00:16:24,483
A BITE OF CHEONGYANG PEPPER

456
00:16:24,550 --> 00:16:26,085
-That's spicy.
-It must be spicy.

457
00:16:26,151 --> 00:16:27,786
That's a disaster.

458
00:16:27,853 --> 00:16:29,455
Wait, is this Cheongyang pepper?

459
00:16:29,521 --> 00:16:30,656
It is.

460
00:16:30,723 --> 00:16:31,890
It doesn't taste spicy.

461
00:16:31,957 --> 00:16:33,926
-"Crunchy Cheongyang pepper."
-It's not spicy.

462
00:16:33,993 --> 00:16:35,995
-It's not spicy at all.
-Not at all.

463
00:16:36,061 --> 00:16:37,062
It's spicy.

464
00:16:37,129 --> 00:16:38,263
She says it's spicy.

465
00:16:38,330 --> 00:16:40,299
-May I smell it? It's not spicy?
-No.

466
00:16:40,366 --> 00:16:41,233
SNIFF

467
00:16:41,300 --> 00:16:42,601
It smells pungent.

468
00:16:42,668 --> 00:16:43,602
It's not a big deal?

469
00:16:44,203 --> 00:16:45,304
POONG GIVES IT A TRY

470
00:16:45,371 --> 00:16:46,772
Still, it looks like

471
00:16:46,839 --> 00:16:48,807
that family has a higher spice tolerance.

472
00:16:50,409 --> 00:16:51,944
It's spicy.

473
00:16:52,011 --> 00:16:54,313
It's the spicy kind. It's really spicy.

474
00:16:54,380 --> 00:16:55,948
Why don't we move on to these?

475
00:16:56,015 --> 00:16:57,716
-Yes, I'd love to.
-Really?

476
00:16:57,783 --> 00:16:59,318
-Get some water ready.
-Okay.

477
00:16:59,385 --> 00:17:00,786
-It's…
-Is it that spicy?

478
00:17:00,853 --> 00:17:03,155
ONE BIRD'S EYE CHILI EACH

479
00:17:04,089 --> 00:17:05,357
SUNG-JU HAS TRIED IT IN EPISODE 10

480
00:17:05,424 --> 00:17:07,059
WHOA!

481
00:17:07,126 --> 00:17:09,261
HE COULDN'T STAND THE SPICINESS

482
00:17:09,328 --> 00:17:10,829
-It's my first time.
-It's spicy.

483
00:17:10,896 --> 00:17:14,199
HOW WILL JONATHAN & PATRICIA TAKE IT?

484
00:17:14,266 --> 00:17:16,769
What's Vietnamese chili like?

485
00:17:16,835 --> 00:17:19,405
-It's not a big deal.
-What?

486
00:17:19,471 --> 00:17:21,573
-You have to chew on it.
-No, I'm serious.

487
00:17:21,640 --> 00:17:23,742
-It's not that spicy. Really.
-No?

488
00:17:23,809 --> 00:17:25,544
-Those are spicy.
-They're really spicy.

489
00:17:25,611 --> 00:17:28,380
-Those are spicy. Oh, my eyes.
-They're really spicy.

490
00:17:28,714 --> 00:17:30,616
IN A RUSH

491
00:17:30,682 --> 00:17:32,684
It's just pleasantly spicy.

492
00:17:32,751 --> 00:17:34,920
-It feels like Cheongyang pepper.
-It does.

493
00:17:34,987 --> 00:17:37,189
IT'S COMING…

494
00:17:37,256 --> 00:17:38,490
BIBI's feeling it.

495
00:17:38,557 --> 00:17:40,759
-Are you okay?
-Are you?

496
00:17:40,826 --> 00:17:43,796
She's good with spicy food
but not compared to them.

497
00:17:43,862 --> 00:17:45,798
-What's wrong? Water?
-What is it?

498
00:17:45,864 --> 00:17:48,367
What are you looking for? Milk.

499
00:17:48,434 --> 00:17:51,403
-Her mouth is burning.
-BIBI is on the weaker side.

500
00:17:51,470 --> 00:17:53,172
-Did you check the expiry date?
-No idea.

501
00:17:53,472 --> 00:17:54,873
BEGINS TO GULP

502
00:17:54,940 --> 00:17:58,077
These two seem completely fine.

503
00:17:58,143 --> 00:18:01,046
-Give us something spicier.
-They're tough.

504
00:18:01,113 --> 00:18:02,781
What's here?

505
00:18:02,848 --> 00:18:04,216
<i>Mala xiang guo </i>sauce.

506
00:18:04,283 --> 00:18:07,219
INGREDIENTS FROM BIBI & NAKYOUNG'S FRIDGE:
MALA XIANG GUO SAUCE

507
00:18:07,286 --> 00:18:09,988
-We make <i>malatang </i>at home.
-We love <i>mala.</i>

508
00:18:10,055 --> 00:18:13,058
And the stir-fry.
Even hot pot. We like to dip things.

509
00:18:13,125 --> 00:18:16,662
We like everything pungent
like chili, onion, garlic,

510
00:18:16,728 --> 00:18:18,063
and green onion.

511
00:18:18,130 --> 00:18:20,332
-The spicy and tangy things.
-And wasabi.

512
00:18:20,399 --> 00:18:22,000
Buldak sauce.

513
00:18:22,067 --> 00:18:25,204
They have lots of spicy sauces
on this side.

514
00:18:25,270 --> 00:18:26,972
Hot sauce.

515
00:18:27,039 --> 00:18:29,007
I like the feeling
when your tongue goes numb.

516
00:18:29,074 --> 00:18:30,809
Something stimulating.

517
00:18:30,876 --> 00:18:32,010
What's this? <i>Meljeot.</i>

518
00:18:32,077 --> 00:18:33,512
Three tubs of anchovy <i>aekjeot.</i>

519
00:18:33,579 --> 00:18:36,381
-We love <i>jeotgal.</i>
-I love all <i>jeotgal.</i>

520
00:18:36,448 --> 00:18:38,217
These are quite salty.

521
00:18:38,283 --> 00:18:40,586
I just put that on rice and eat it.

522
00:18:40,652 --> 00:18:42,321
-We do that.
-Over white rice?

523
00:18:42,387 --> 00:18:44,189
It's amazing with pork belly.

524
00:18:44,256 --> 00:18:45,290
That's why we have it.

525
00:18:45,357 --> 00:18:48,894
It's really good
when mixed with pepper and garlic.

526
00:18:49,461 --> 00:18:51,630
I think she has kimchi on this side.

527
00:18:51,697 --> 00:18:54,366
-Green onion kimchi?
-I recently got it from someone.

528
00:18:54,433 --> 00:18:55,601
Who gave it to you?

529
00:18:55,667 --> 00:18:57,536
This old lady that I know.

530
00:18:57,603 --> 00:18:59,338
-An old lady that you know?
-Do you like this?

531
00:18:59,404 --> 00:19:00,606
I love green onion.

532
00:19:00,672 --> 00:19:03,175
I need that to eat with Chapaghetti.

533
00:19:03,242 --> 00:19:06,345
-I can't live without kimchi.
-She can't.

534
00:19:06,411 --> 00:19:07,846
The third shelf.

535
00:19:07,913 --> 00:19:09,248
I see Western ingredients.

536
00:19:09,314 --> 00:19:11,550
She has lots of Western ingredients
like cheese.

537
00:19:11,617 --> 00:19:14,052
You have a variety of cheese.

538
00:19:14,119 --> 00:19:15,654
What's this?

539
00:19:15,721 --> 00:19:19,691
-It's called heart of palm.
-Yes, it says that.

540
00:19:19,758 --> 00:19:21,593
-Heart of palm.
-Like pickles?

541
00:19:21,660 --> 00:19:24,029
-From a palm tree.
-The core of a palm tree.

542
00:19:24,096 --> 00:19:26,165
It's the core of a palm tree.

543
00:19:26,231 --> 00:19:28,066
Can this be used as food?

544
00:19:28,133 --> 00:19:30,936
It's considered
a premium ingredient in Western cuisine.

545
00:19:31,003 --> 00:19:33,172
What does it taste like?
Or is it mostly for texture?

546
00:19:33,238 --> 00:19:34,640
It's soft yet crunchy.

547
00:19:34,706 --> 00:19:35,974
-Like bamboo shoots?
-Like artichokes.

548
00:19:36,041 --> 00:19:37,976
Yes, and bamboo shoots.

549
00:19:38,043 --> 00:19:40,045
What's this? Guanciale?

550
00:19:40,112 --> 00:19:42,614
-She has guanciale?
-You have lots of Western ingredients.

551
00:19:42,681 --> 00:19:43,582
She has guanciale?

552
00:19:43,649 --> 00:19:45,784
Yes, because I love carbonara pasta.

553
00:19:45,851 --> 00:19:47,586
Gosh, seriously.

554
00:19:47,653 --> 00:19:49,154
-Actual carbonara pasta.
-Guanciale?

555
00:19:49,221 --> 00:19:51,290
Not the fake ones. The real deal.

556
00:19:51,356 --> 00:19:54,359
People often use bacon, but you need
guanciale to make the real one.

557
00:19:54,426 --> 00:19:56,995
-And tomato sauce.
-I see.

558
00:19:57,062 --> 00:20:00,232
Do you make pasta often?
I see lots of noodles here.

559
00:20:00,299 --> 00:20:02,868
INGREDIENTS FROM BIBI & NAKYOUNG'S FRIDGE:
PASTA NOODLES

560
00:20:02,935 --> 00:20:04,536
We make pasta often.

561
00:20:04,603 --> 00:20:07,139
It's easy to make. Just toss it.

562
00:20:07,206 --> 00:20:08,674
-It's easy to make?
-It's easy.

563
00:20:08,740 --> 00:20:10,809
What do people say about your pasta?

564
00:20:10,876 --> 00:20:13,278
It's really good. Whenever she makes it,

565
00:20:13,345 --> 00:20:14,913
it's seasoned perfectly.

566
00:20:14,980 --> 00:20:16,915
She has a knack for cooking.

567
00:20:16,982 --> 00:20:19,751
When you watched our show avidly,

568
00:20:19,818 --> 00:20:22,688
-did you try our recipes too?
-I often did. Thanks for your service.

569
00:20:22,988 --> 00:20:24,089
FEELING PROUD

570
00:20:24,156 --> 00:20:25,190
"Thanks for your service."

571
00:20:25,257 --> 00:20:26,758
She watched us and learned to cook.

572
00:20:26,825 --> 00:20:27,893
A singer <i>Chef & My Fridge </i>has raised.

573
00:20:29,661 --> 00:20:31,530
A singer whom chefs have brought up.

574
00:20:32,097 --> 00:20:33,565
I see some cutlassfish.

575
00:20:33,632 --> 00:20:34,967
CUTLASSFISH

576
00:20:35,033 --> 00:20:35,968
Stalked sea squirt.

577
00:20:36,034 --> 00:20:38,437
-I love stalked sea squirt.
-Those are for NaKyoung.

578
00:20:38,704 --> 00:20:39,871
AN UNEXPECTED TASTE IN FOOD

579
00:20:39,938 --> 00:20:42,307
I love the smell so much.

580
00:20:42,374 --> 00:20:44,309
-I love it too.
-I hate stalked sea squirt.

581
00:20:44,376 --> 00:20:47,312
When you order fish soup for delivery,

582
00:20:47,379 --> 00:20:49,615
there's stalked sea squirt inside, right?

583
00:20:49,681 --> 00:20:51,783
-I eat it all.
-Stalked sea squirt?

584
00:20:51,850 --> 00:20:52,818
It pops in your mouth.

585
00:20:52,884 --> 00:20:54,152
Do you eat the shell too?

586
00:20:54,219 --> 00:20:56,121
I eat it all. I swallow it whole.

587
00:20:56,188 --> 00:20:59,591
You lived in Busan and Ulsan,
so you must love seafood.

588
00:20:59,658 --> 00:21:03,795
-Yes, seafood was a staple.
-Mostly seafood.

589
00:21:03,862 --> 00:21:05,664
Raw conger eel.

590
00:21:05,731 --> 00:21:08,567
-Steamed monkfish.
-Steamed monkfish. We ate that so often.

591
00:21:08,634 --> 00:21:10,535
Steamed monkfish? I've never had it.

592
00:21:10,602 --> 00:21:12,271
-My dad fishes.
-Your dad fishes?

593
00:21:12,337 --> 00:21:14,273
-Out in the sea?
-Does he still do it?

594
00:21:14,339 --> 00:21:15,707
Yes, and I tag along.

595
00:21:15,774 --> 00:21:17,042
I see.

596
00:21:17,109 --> 00:21:19,711
-And grab a drink of soju?
-Of course.

597
00:21:19,778 --> 00:21:21,380
I see it right here.

598
00:21:21,446 --> 00:21:24,049
-You have the red caps, stronger ones.
-The red caps.

599
00:21:24,116 --> 00:21:26,084
-Isn't it simply more delicious?
-It's stronger.

600
00:21:26,151 --> 00:21:28,253
I always thought
the red caps tasted better.

601
00:21:28,320 --> 00:21:30,389
In the end, alcohol itself
doesn't taste that good.

602
00:21:30,455 --> 00:21:31,790
-Right.
-But you can

603
00:21:31,857 --> 00:21:34,293
get drunk more easily
with the same amount.

604
00:21:34,359 --> 00:21:35,360
Isn't that a win?

605
00:21:35,427 --> 00:21:37,496
-More efficient?
-It's not about the taste.

606
00:21:37,562 --> 00:21:39,831
That's what adults thought
in the old days.

607
00:21:39,898 --> 00:21:42,434
So I like my drinks
up in the forty-something proof.

608
00:21:42,501 --> 00:21:43,669
How much alcohol can you take?

609
00:21:43,735 --> 00:21:45,270
One bottle of the red cap.

610
00:21:45,337 --> 00:21:46,505
-One bottle.
-And NaKyoung?

611
00:21:46,571 --> 00:21:49,207
One bottle of red cap,
and for the green one, two.

612
00:21:50,142 --> 00:21:51,710
-They can take their alcohol.
-You're tough.

613
00:21:51,777 --> 00:21:53,412
That's tough. How scary.

614
00:21:53,478 --> 00:21:55,447
And down here,

615
00:21:55,514 --> 00:21:58,817
some vegetables. I see some lettuce.

616
00:21:58,884 --> 00:22:00,485
And water parsley? Do you like these?

617
00:22:00,552 --> 00:22:01,853
-She loves them.
-Those are mine.

618
00:22:01,920 --> 00:22:03,955
I love water parsley so much.

619
00:22:04,022 --> 00:22:05,524
-Don't you love the smell?
-It's great.

620
00:22:05,590 --> 00:22:07,192
It's great with braised monkfish.

621
00:22:07,259 --> 00:22:08,593
It's a must.

622
00:22:08,660 --> 00:22:11,797
So they like veggies too.
Now, down here in the freezer.

623
00:22:12,264 --> 00:22:14,032
I see lots of seafood.

624
00:22:14,099 --> 00:22:17,369
-Mackerel. Cutlassfish. Shrimp.
-Shrimp.

625
00:22:17,436 --> 00:22:19,671
But these aren't prepped.

626
00:22:19,738 --> 00:22:21,573
I love the texture of it,

627
00:22:21,640 --> 00:22:23,709
so I always eat the heads and the tails

628
00:22:23,775 --> 00:22:26,678
while NaKyoung eats the meat.

629
00:22:26,745 --> 00:22:27,846
When I was younger,

630
00:22:27,913 --> 00:22:30,816
I had this idea
that she was only into the tails.

631
00:22:30,882 --> 00:22:32,984
So I'd eat the meat
and give her only the tails.

632
00:22:33,051 --> 00:22:35,954
But I still need
a bit of meat on it, right?

633
00:22:36,021 --> 00:22:37,923
But she had eaten every bit of the flesh.

634
00:22:37,989 --> 00:22:40,125
She thought you only liked the tails.

635
00:22:40,192 --> 00:22:41,626
But it was so cute, though.

636
00:22:41,693 --> 00:22:43,161
-Here.
-She'd be eating over there,

637
00:22:43,228 --> 00:22:46,865
-then she'd run up and go, "BIBI, tails."
-"BIBI loves tails."

638
00:22:46,932 --> 00:22:49,768
She often gave me the tails like that.

639
00:22:49,835 --> 00:22:51,236
-She went that far?
-Yes.

640
00:22:51,303 --> 00:22:53,605
So I like food with a good texture.

641
00:22:53,672 --> 00:22:55,107
What about fried chicken?

642
00:22:55,173 --> 00:22:59,211
As for the drumsticks,
I don't eat the meat, but the tips.

643
00:22:59,277 --> 00:23:01,546
-What? Just the tips?
-Just the tips?

644
00:23:01,613 --> 00:23:03,582
-The crunchy part.
-The cartilage.

645
00:23:03,648 --> 00:23:04,816
It's the cartilage.

646
00:23:04,883 --> 00:23:06,284
I only like the cartilages.

647
00:23:06,351 --> 00:23:07,753
That's unusual.

648
00:23:07,819 --> 00:23:10,889
-I hope they sell just the cartilages.
-Fried cartilages.

649
00:23:10,956 --> 00:23:12,657
I guess you'd never fight over food.

650
00:23:12,724 --> 00:23:14,092
-You both like different things.
-Right.

651
00:23:14,159 --> 00:23:15,360
What about you two?

652
00:23:15,427 --> 00:23:17,863
It's like the remote control incident.
It's the same logic.

653
00:23:17,929 --> 00:23:20,699
-I have to go for it first.
-Whoever gets it wins.

654
00:23:20,766 --> 00:23:21,700
The drumsticks?

655
00:23:21,767 --> 00:23:23,735
When the delivery comes,

656
00:23:23,802 --> 00:23:26,671
it's usually the person
who goes out to get it.

657
00:23:26,738 --> 00:23:29,775
Yes, that's the winner.
We grab it outside the door.

658
00:23:29,841 --> 00:23:32,110
-We tamper with it first.
-Tamper.

659
00:23:32,177 --> 00:23:33,478
You're competing all the time.

660
00:23:33,545 --> 00:23:36,281
-Every moment. We never get a break.
-It's a battle.

661
00:23:36,348 --> 00:23:37,716
Next, the meat.

662
00:23:37,783 --> 00:23:39,251
-Beef.
-Beef.

663
00:23:39,317 --> 00:23:40,719
Pork belly.

664
00:23:40,786 --> 00:23:43,221
-You have lots of pork skin.
-What's this?

665
00:23:43,288 --> 00:23:44,589
Pork skin sounds so good!

666
00:23:44,656 --> 00:23:47,192
A BATCH OF PORK FAT

667
00:23:47,259 --> 00:23:49,728
I watched this YouTuber

668
00:23:49,795 --> 00:23:51,696
enjoying pork fat so much.

669
00:23:51,763 --> 00:23:54,065
-Just the fat? It's good.
-Yes.

670
00:23:54,132 --> 00:23:56,001
So I bought it, thinking it was one chunk,

671
00:23:56,067 --> 00:23:57,536
but I got three.

672
00:23:57,602 --> 00:24:00,272
I now have no idea what to do with that.

673
00:24:00,338 --> 00:24:01,573
Why don't you…

674
00:24:01,640 --> 00:24:03,775
-You can always give it to us.
-You can give it to us.

675
00:24:04,843 --> 00:24:07,279
-Tell us how to eat it, and we'll do that.
-We got it.

676
00:24:07,345 --> 00:24:10,782
-Are you asking the chefs to cook with it?
-Yes, that would be wonderful.

677
00:24:10,849 --> 00:24:12,617
Shelf-stable products.

678
00:24:12,684 --> 00:24:14,619
You really do love these things.

679
00:24:14,686 --> 00:24:16,822
-Hot pot ingredients.
-Yes.

680
00:24:16,888 --> 00:24:19,758
That's all for BIBI and NaKyoung's fridge!

681
00:24:19,825 --> 00:24:20,992
Wow.

682
00:24:21,059 --> 00:24:23,995
I wonder
what they're looking to eat today.

683
00:24:24,062 --> 00:24:26,965
Let's find out their orders. Show us!

684
00:24:27,532 --> 00:24:30,869
These are BIBI and NaKyoung's orders.

685
00:24:30,936 --> 00:24:32,270
They both popped up together.

686
00:24:32,337 --> 00:24:34,973
Spicy Sisters' Fiery Make-up Food.

687
00:24:35,040 --> 00:24:37,976
-We always make up over <i>tteokbokki.</i>
-Making up.

688
00:24:38,643 --> 00:24:41,813
We want our fight to be forgotten
because the food

689
00:24:41,880 --> 00:24:43,248
is painfully spicy.

690
00:24:44,149 --> 00:24:46,985
So we wanted to ask you to make that.

691
00:24:47,052 --> 00:24:48,353
THE END OF PRESENTATION!

692
00:24:48,420 --> 00:24:50,522
Gyeongsang-do Sisters' Soul Food.

693
00:24:50,589 --> 00:24:54,659
We're sisters who grew up
on the coasts of South Gyeongsang-do,

694
00:24:54,726 --> 00:24:57,629
so having <i>hoe, </i>raw fish fillet,
and soju is our thing.

695
00:24:57,863 --> 00:24:59,498
TRUE THAT!

696
00:24:59,564 --> 00:25:02,868
So I want to ask you to make something
that'll make us go,

697
00:25:02,934 --> 00:25:04,636
-"It's the real deal!"
-I see.

698
00:25:04,703 --> 00:25:06,805
When it comes to sisters,
we got the double high beams.

699
00:25:06,872 --> 00:25:09,040
-You should probably join.
-Yes.

700
00:25:09,107 --> 00:25:10,642
The high beams of Geumjeong-dong are in.

701
00:25:10,709 --> 00:25:12,744
Spicy Sisters' Fiery Make-up Food.

702
00:25:12,811 --> 00:25:17,749
My sister and I
also often had spicy food as we made up.

703
00:25:17,816 --> 00:25:20,352
It's the perfect round for me.

704
00:25:20,418 --> 00:25:23,154
The high beams of Geumjeong-dong
join the first one.

705
00:25:23,221 --> 00:25:25,390
-Ho-young?
-Hey.

706
00:25:25,457 --> 00:25:27,259
I'll make their makeup food.

707
00:25:27,492 --> 00:25:28,827
SURPRISED

708
00:25:28,894 --> 00:25:30,729
-Spicy food?
-I thought he'd go for seafood.

709
00:25:30,795 --> 00:25:32,898
I know I can make a good spicy dish.

710
00:25:32,964 --> 00:25:34,933
I thought you'd go for seafood.

711
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:36,768
Are you good with spicy food?

712
00:25:36,835 --> 00:25:38,436
I never eat my meals

713
00:25:38,503 --> 00:25:40,171
unless I have Cheongyang peppers.

714
00:25:40,238 --> 00:25:41,540
What? Really?

715
00:25:42,807 --> 00:25:45,110
-Ho-young's in.
-He eats well regardless of that.

716
00:25:45,176 --> 00:25:47,412
Four bowls of rice with pepper
and three without.

717
00:25:47,479 --> 00:25:50,882
So the spicy round has been decided.
Eun-yeong versus Ho-young.

718
00:25:50,949 --> 00:25:53,118
Gyeongsang-do Sisters' Soul Food,
the seafood round.

719
00:25:53,184 --> 00:25:54,152
I'll go.

720
00:25:54,920 --> 00:25:55,754
The seafood round?

721
00:25:55,820 --> 00:25:58,056
Whenever I come up with the menu
at my restaurant,

722
00:25:58,123 --> 00:26:00,191
it's about 80 to 90% seafood.

723
00:26:00,525 --> 00:26:02,360
CHOI HYUN-SUK WILL HANDLE THIS ROUND

724
00:26:02,427 --> 00:26:05,096
So Hyun-suk suddenly joins the next round.

725
00:26:05,163 --> 00:26:06,965
Who can beat him?

726
00:26:07,032 --> 00:26:08,066
I will.

727
00:26:08,366 --> 00:26:10,335
RISING EXPECTATIONS

728
00:26:10,402 --> 00:26:13,572
I like the sound of seafood,
and she has the most abundant ingredients.

729
00:26:13,638 --> 00:26:15,073
I couldn't miss the chance.

730
00:26:15,140 --> 00:26:17,275
You should seize your chance
to cook with these.

731
00:26:17,342 --> 00:26:21,112
So the chefs with many stars
joined the second round.

732
00:26:21,179 --> 00:26:22,981
-It's a big match.
-It is.

733
00:26:23,048 --> 00:26:26,985
Let us begin
Eun-yeong and Ho-young's round first.

734
00:26:28,486 --> 00:26:30,255
Go.

735
00:26:30,322 --> 00:26:35,493
<i>The first round of cook-off played with</i>
<i>BIBI and NaKyoung's ingredients.</i>

736
00:26:35,560 --> 00:26:38,496
They're the dancing siblings of the show.

737
00:26:38,563 --> 00:26:41,600
The dancing machine,
the older brother, Chef Jung Ho-young,

738
00:26:41,666 --> 00:26:46,004
versus the ball of lunacy,
the younger sister, Chef Park Eun-yeong.

739
00:26:46,071 --> 00:26:49,975
We have a record of something
that Eun-yeong has said.

740
00:26:50,041 --> 00:26:54,946
"Someone like Chef Ho-young
is no longer in my league."

741
00:26:55,013 --> 00:26:55,947
TAUNTING AGGRESSIVELY

742
00:26:56,014 --> 00:26:57,816
"Someone like that"?

743
00:26:57,882 --> 00:27:00,685
-Yes.
-Ho-young and I are often paired as rivals

744
00:27:00,752 --> 00:27:02,621
because we both dance often.

745
00:27:02,687 --> 00:27:04,122
But take a look

746
00:27:04,189 --> 00:27:06,191
at Chef Ho-young's moves,
which are heavy and clumsy.

747
00:27:06,257 --> 00:27:08,159
-"Heavy and clumsy"?
-"Heavy and clumsy"?

748
00:27:08,226 --> 00:27:10,495
I don't think he's in my league.

749
00:27:10,562 --> 00:27:12,063
A CHEF DISCUSSING DANCE SKILLS

750
00:27:12,130 --> 00:27:14,432
His moves aren't so sharp.

751
00:27:14,499 --> 00:27:17,168
<i>I wouldn't say his moves are accurate.</i>

752
00:27:17,969 --> 00:27:19,638
She became kind of smug

753
00:27:19,704 --> 00:27:23,475
after going viral with that one thing,
the Meatball Queen.

754
00:27:24,109 --> 00:27:26,845
But I've spent many years dancing.

755
00:27:26,911 --> 00:27:29,247
-Of course.
-If she wants to beat me,

756
00:27:29,314 --> 00:27:30,749
she needs a few more years.

757
00:27:30,815 --> 00:27:32,584
He's right about that.

758
00:27:32,651 --> 00:27:35,787
<i>He has been dancing since the days</i>
<i>when people said, "What's wrong with him?"</i>

759
00:27:35,854 --> 00:27:38,089
<i>People were like, "Why does he do that?"</i>

760
00:27:38,156 --> 00:27:40,325
-Back when no chefs ever danced.
-Exactly.

761
00:27:40,392 --> 00:27:42,694
-He danced back then.
-He started back then.

762
00:27:42,761 --> 00:27:45,196
-He has paved the way.
-That's true.

763
00:27:45,263 --> 00:27:50,101
They're arguing that Eun-yeong got here
because Ho-young started it all.

764
00:27:50,168 --> 00:27:52,070
What's more urgent at this point

765
00:27:52,137 --> 00:27:55,273
is for you two to make peace
instead of BIBI and NaKyoung.

766
00:27:55,340 --> 00:27:58,109
But it doesn't look like
Eun-yeong wants to make up with him.

767
00:27:58,176 --> 00:27:59,844
Not only does she think
she's a better dancer,

768
00:27:59,911 --> 00:28:02,747
but she also thinks
the order is pretty much for her.

769
00:28:02,814 --> 00:28:06,151
I know very well
how sisters make up over spicy food

770
00:28:06,217 --> 00:28:09,087
through my 30 years of experience
with my sister.

771
00:28:09,154 --> 00:28:13,158
I really know that all I have to do
is make something very spicy for them.

772
00:28:13,224 --> 00:28:14,993
After all, Ho-young can't be sisters.

773
00:28:15,060 --> 00:28:16,594
I can't be sisters with someone,

774
00:28:16,661 --> 00:28:18,797
but I really love spicy food.

775
00:28:18,863 --> 00:28:21,032
I pop Cheongyang peppers
into my mouth like popcorn.

776
00:28:21,099 --> 00:28:23,401
I always keep it by my side.

777
00:28:23,468 --> 00:28:25,570
I'd chew on one while watching TV.

778
00:28:25,637 --> 00:28:27,806
He went way too far.

779
00:28:27,872 --> 00:28:29,541
His pride in spice tolerance is a lie.

780
00:28:30,608 --> 00:28:32,544
PICKING UP A CHEONGYANG PEPPER?

781
00:28:34,579 --> 00:28:35,780
Is he serious?

782
00:28:36,681 --> 00:28:37,782
WATCH ME

783
00:28:37,849 --> 00:28:41,019
Hey, your face is turning red!

784
00:28:41,086 --> 00:28:42,153
His cheeks are red.

785
00:28:42,220 --> 00:28:43,054
BUT…

786
00:28:43,621 --> 00:28:46,091
He's proving that he wasn't lying.

787
00:28:46,391 --> 00:28:48,827
WHY DOES HE TAKE THINGS THAT FAR?

788
00:28:48,893 --> 00:28:50,595
It must taste spicy.

789
00:28:50,662 --> 00:28:52,630
-It must be.
-No, he doesn't find it spicy.

790
00:28:52,697 --> 00:28:56,501
He has a hobby of whistling
after eating Cheongyang pepper.

791
00:28:57,535 --> 00:28:58,937
He likes to whistle?

792
00:28:59,003 --> 00:29:00,772
-It must be spicy.
-No, he likes to whistle.

793
00:29:00,839 --> 00:29:02,640
-That's his hobby.
-Whistling.

794
00:29:02,707 --> 00:29:03,975
A QUITE NICHE HOBBY

795
00:29:04,042 --> 00:29:06,010
-It looks spicy.
-It's not that it's spicy.

796
00:29:06,077 --> 00:29:07,879
No, it's not spicy at all.

797
00:29:07,946 --> 00:29:10,882
He has a habit of whistling
after eating Cheongyang peppers.

798
00:29:11,349 --> 00:29:13,585
CHEF EUN-YEONG'S INGREDIENTS:
PORK FAT, SOJU, BAY LEAF, BEECH MUSHROOM

799
00:29:13,651 --> 00:29:15,887
CABBAGE, WATER PARSLEY, CHEONGYANG PEPPER,
MALA SEASONING OIL, OYSTER SAUCE, AND MORE

800
00:29:15,954 --> 00:29:18,356
What are you making, Chef Eun-yeong?

801
00:29:18,423 --> 00:29:20,024
The name of my dish is…

802
00:29:20,258 --> 00:29:21,626
HIGH HOPES

803
00:29:21,693 --> 00:29:23,161
…Red Flavor, Curious, <i>Mala.</i>

804
00:29:23,228 --> 00:29:25,497
It seemed like
you two were into the taste of <i>mala</i>.

805
00:29:25,563 --> 00:29:28,266
You had lots of ingredients
from Sichuan in the fridge,

806
00:29:28,333 --> 00:29:31,202
so I want to make
one of Sichuan's famous dishes,

807
00:29:31,269 --> 00:29:33,071
Sichuan water-poached meat, <i>shuizhu </i>beef.

808
00:29:33,138 --> 00:29:34,973
-Water-poached beef.
-Yes.

809
00:29:35,039 --> 00:29:38,977
It's a dish where you boil meat
in spicy and hot soup.

810
00:29:39,043 --> 00:29:40,779
BIBI also mentioned

811
00:29:40,845 --> 00:29:43,448
she couldn't find good use
for the pork fat,

812
00:29:43,515 --> 00:29:45,283
-Yes.
-…which came with the skin.

813
00:29:45,350 --> 00:29:46,918
I'll boil the pork skin nicely

814
00:29:46,985 --> 00:29:50,622
and make a spicy salad
using NaKyoung's favorite, water parsley.

815
00:29:50,688 --> 00:29:53,391
-A spicy set meal, so to speak.
-It also came with skin?

816
00:29:54,325 --> 00:29:56,327
CHEF HO-YOUNG'S INGREDIENTS:
TENDERLOIN, PORK BELLY, SWEET PUMPKIN

817
00:29:56,394 --> 00:29:58,396
STALKED SEA SQUIRT, BIRD'S EYE CHILI,
CHEONGYANG PEPPER, POLLACK ROE, AND MORE

818
00:29:58,463 --> 00:30:00,932
What are you making, Ho-young?

819
00:30:00,999 --> 00:30:02,567
Though you didn't have short ribs,

820
00:30:02,634 --> 00:30:05,370
I'll use pork belly and beef tenderloin

821
00:30:05,436 --> 00:30:07,005
to make spicy <i>galbi-jjim,</i>
braised short ribs,

822
00:30:07,071 --> 00:30:10,175
with green onion kimchi.

823
00:30:10,241 --> 00:30:12,343
-And then,
-Green onion kimchi?

824
00:30:12,410 --> 00:30:15,246
…NaKyoung likes stalked sea squirt,
so I'll make a bibimbap with it.

825
00:30:15,313 --> 00:30:17,215
-And…
-Stalked sea squirt bibimbap?

826
00:30:17,282 --> 00:30:20,018
-I've never heard of it.
-…I'll turn them into tiny rice balls

827
00:30:20,084 --> 00:30:23,655
to make it easier to eat with <i>galbi-jjim.</i>

828
00:30:24,889 --> 00:30:26,724
In just 15 minutes?

829
00:30:29,427 --> 00:30:30,528
HIGH FIVE

830
00:30:30,595 --> 00:30:33,498
Let us begin the round,
"Spicy Sisters' Fiery Make-up Food."

831
00:30:33,565 --> 00:30:35,366
Give us the timer for 15 minutes.

832
00:30:35,433 --> 00:30:36,835
-Whoa.
-Whoa.

833
00:30:36,901 --> 00:30:38,570
-This is legendary.
-No way.

834
00:30:38,636 --> 00:30:40,405
We give them only 15 minutes.

835
00:30:40,471 --> 00:30:41,840
It's really 15 minutes.

836
00:30:41,906 --> 00:30:44,509
Here to take down Ho-young
with her experience

837
00:30:44,576 --> 00:30:46,911
from fighting her older sister,

838
00:30:46,978 --> 00:30:49,514
the high beams of Geumjeong-dong,
Chef Park Eun-yeong.

839
00:30:49,581 --> 00:30:52,717
Here to make Eun-yeong cry a river,

840
00:30:52,784 --> 00:30:56,921
the fiery spatula of Yeonhui-dong,
Chef Jung Ho-young.

841
00:30:56,988 --> 00:31:00,191
Which one of them will capture
the spicy sisters' appetite?

842
00:31:00,258 --> 00:31:03,895
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

843
00:31:05,730 --> 00:31:07,098
ALREADY FIRED UP IN EXCITEMENT

844
00:31:07,632 --> 00:31:10,034
THIS ROUND IS SO HOT

845
00:31:10,101 --> 00:31:12,070
Make something spicy for them.

846
00:31:12,136 --> 00:31:13,304
You can see everything.

847
00:31:13,371 --> 00:31:14,839
-We can see it.
-The timer has started.

848
00:31:14,906 --> 00:31:16,341
We can actually see them.

849
00:31:16,407 --> 00:31:17,909
You begin just like that?

850
00:31:17,976 --> 00:31:19,377
It was the real deal.

851
00:31:19,944 --> 00:31:21,346
I have to do well today.

852
00:31:21,412 --> 00:31:23,581
-She's grabbing the pork fat.
-Right away?

853
00:31:23,648 --> 00:31:26,384
-Yes, the pork fat.
-She has to remove the skin.

854
00:31:27,552 --> 00:31:29,354
Her knife looks so cool.
I've never seen that.

855
00:31:29,420 --> 00:31:31,489
-It's a Chinese kitchen knife.
-Really?

856
00:31:31,556 --> 00:31:33,758
She's removing the pork skin.

857
00:31:33,825 --> 00:31:35,426
So she's making salad with that?

858
00:31:35,493 --> 00:31:37,629
Yes, it's delicious
once you boil it and season it.

859
00:31:37,695 --> 00:31:39,497
My gosh.

860
00:31:39,564 --> 00:31:41,232
-It won't be easy.
-In 15 minutes?

861
00:31:41,299 --> 00:31:42,200
It'll be tough.

862
00:31:42,267 --> 00:31:45,270
She's doing that first
because it takes time to cook.

863
00:31:46,037 --> 00:31:48,873
She poured soju into the pot
to remove the fat smell.

864
00:31:49,073 --> 00:31:51,009
WOW!

865
00:31:51,309 --> 00:31:52,343
BEGINS WITH THE TENDERLOIN

866
00:31:52,410 --> 00:31:53,912
He's prepping the meat first.

867
00:31:54,379 --> 00:31:58,983
1. CHOP TENDERLOIN
INTO BITE-SIZED PIECES AND GRILL

868
00:31:59,183 --> 00:32:00,285
WHAT'S NEXT?

869
00:32:00,351 --> 00:32:01,819
Ho-young grabs pork belly.

870
00:32:01,886 --> 00:32:03,554
-I love pork belly.
-Beef and pork?

871
00:32:03,621 --> 00:32:05,723
I'm using pork belly.

872
00:32:05,790 --> 00:32:09,193
He's going to braise it,
so he threw it into a pressure cooker.

873
00:32:09,360 --> 00:32:11,963
ONION, GREEN ONION, GARLIC

874
00:32:12,030 --> 00:32:13,264
He's so fast.

875
00:32:13,698 --> 00:32:14,666
INTO THE COOKER

876
00:32:14,732 --> 00:32:17,135
-Sweet pumpkin.
-Sweet pumpkin.

877
00:32:17,201 --> 00:32:20,505
My makeup artist's parents
grew that themselves.

878
00:32:20,571 --> 00:32:22,240
I see.

879
00:32:22,307 --> 00:32:24,909
-It's really good.
-They grew that themselves.

880
00:32:24,976 --> 00:32:27,278
-"Sweet, oh so sweet, sweet pumpkin."
-You're right.

881
00:32:27,345 --> 00:32:30,214
Sweet, oh so sweet, sweet pumpkin.

882
00:32:30,481 --> 00:32:31,783
PORK SKIN, OUT

883
00:32:31,849 --> 00:32:33,651
-Look at that pork skin.
-It's cooked already?

884
00:32:33,718 --> 00:32:36,020
-I'll cook it in a pressure cooker.
-A pressure cooker.

885
00:32:36,087 --> 00:32:39,090
-I see. The skin.
-Right. If she uses a pressure cooker,

886
00:32:39,157 --> 00:32:41,025
it'll get rid of that fat smell.

887
00:32:41,092 --> 00:32:43,861
She's making use of a pressure cooker.

888
00:32:43,928 --> 00:32:46,397
She's steaming pork skin
in the pressure cooker.

889
00:32:46,597 --> 00:32:47,532
BEECH MUSHROOM

890
00:32:47,598 --> 00:32:49,767
I love mushrooms.
Especially oyster mushrooms.

891
00:32:49,834 --> 00:32:51,035
Eun-yeong grabs mushrooms

892
00:32:51,102 --> 00:32:52,971
-and cabbage.
-Cabbage.

893
00:32:54,439 --> 00:32:57,542
Are you putting the cabbage
underneath the pork?

894
00:32:57,608 --> 00:32:58,977
I'm putting it into the hot pot.

895
00:32:59,043 --> 00:33:00,178
Into the hot pot.

896
00:33:00,244 --> 00:33:01,079
PREPPING THE VEGGIES

897
00:33:01,145 --> 00:33:03,481
It's been three minutes.

898
00:33:03,548 --> 00:33:04,849
Wow.

899
00:33:04,916 --> 00:33:06,651
Ho-young is making sauce.

900
00:33:06,718 --> 00:33:07,618
OYSTER SAUCE

901
00:33:07,685 --> 00:33:09,187
SUGAR, VINEGAR

902
00:33:09,253 --> 00:33:10,888
He's adding lots of red pepper powder.

903
00:33:11,456 --> 00:33:13,558
-That looks spicy.
-It does.

904
00:33:15,093 --> 00:33:16,928
He's pouring the sauce in there.

905
00:33:16,995 --> 00:33:19,931
It looks so red.

906
00:33:19,998 --> 00:33:22,000
-Whoa. It looks spicy.
-It looks so spicy.

907
00:33:22,300 --> 00:33:23,935
GETTING EXCITED

908
00:33:24,002 --> 00:33:25,169
SUDDEN DISTANCE

909
00:33:25,837 --> 00:33:27,305
Are you braising that too?

910
00:33:27,372 --> 00:33:30,141
Yes, I'll have the pork belly
braised so it's tender,

911
00:33:30,208 --> 00:33:33,544
then top it with grilled beef tenderloin.

912
00:33:33,611 --> 00:33:34,512
It smells so good.

913
00:33:35,446 --> 00:33:36,547
-Water parsley.
-Water parsley?

914
00:33:36,614 --> 00:33:38,282
-NaKyoung's favorite.
-Water parsley.

915
00:33:38,349 --> 00:33:40,785
NaKyoung's favorite, water parsley.

916
00:33:40,852 --> 00:33:42,220
-It's Eun-yeong.
-Water parsley.

917
00:33:43,221 --> 00:33:44,555
Cheongyang pepper.

918
00:33:44,622 --> 00:33:45,490
TO ADD SPICINESS

919
00:33:45,556 --> 00:33:46,758
Yes, add some chili peppers.

920
00:33:47,058 --> 00:33:50,161
4. PREP WATER PARSLEY, CHEONGYANG PEPPER,
GARLIC, THEN MIX WITH MALA OIL

921
00:33:50,228 --> 00:33:52,563
-That one has to be good.
-It has to be.

922
00:33:52,630 --> 00:33:53,798
They're both so quick.

923
00:33:53,865 --> 00:33:54,999
They really are.

924
00:33:55,066 --> 00:33:56,968
Stalked sea squirt…

925
00:33:57,035 --> 00:33:58,403
-Do you need help?
-A bit.

926
00:33:59,103 --> 00:34:00,471
ON MY WAY

927
00:34:01,806 --> 00:34:03,574
What are you up to now, Sam?

928
00:34:03,641 --> 00:34:06,044
-He just took something up.
-Prepping the stalked sea squirt.

929
00:34:06,110 --> 00:34:07,712
He's cutting off the tips.

930
00:34:07,779 --> 00:34:10,014
-Removing the innards.
-One at a time?

931
00:34:10,081 --> 00:34:12,350
Ho-young said
he'd make bibimbap with that.

932
00:34:12,417 --> 00:34:14,419
I've never had stalked sea squirt
out of the shell.

933
00:34:14,485 --> 00:34:16,120
-I see.
-Shell-less stalked sea squirt.

934
00:34:16,187 --> 00:34:17,321
Shell-less stalked sea squirt.

935
00:34:17,555 --> 00:34:18,956
SLAM

936
00:34:19,023 --> 00:34:19,991
She's so quick.

937
00:34:20,825 --> 00:34:22,894
DESTROYING GARLIC

938
00:34:22,960 --> 00:34:25,763
-I've never seen anything like it.
-She's crushing the garlic.

939
00:34:25,830 --> 00:34:27,198
-How cool.
-Lots of garlic.

940
00:34:28,066 --> 00:34:29,033
Green onion.

941
00:34:29,867 --> 00:34:31,269
How is she so good with the knife?

942
00:34:31,903 --> 00:34:33,571
And she's really fast.

943
00:34:33,638 --> 00:34:35,640
-I'd cut off my hand.
-At that speed, she must be

944
00:34:35,706 --> 00:34:37,008
willing to sacrifice her hand.

945
00:34:37,475 --> 00:34:38,376
DON'T BE SO SURPRISED YET

946
00:34:38,443 --> 00:34:41,446
-There it is. <i>Mala </i>sauce.
-<i>Mala </i>sauce!

947
00:34:41,512 --> 00:34:43,181
-The red flavor!
-Eun-yeong!

948
00:34:43,247 --> 00:34:44,315
RED FLAVOR

949
00:34:44,382 --> 00:34:45,450
Red flavor.

950
00:34:45,516 --> 00:34:47,251
Eun-yeong's red flavor.

951
00:34:47,318 --> 00:34:49,020
I'm smelling the <i>mala </i>now.

952
00:34:49,087 --> 00:34:50,154
It's dark red.

953
00:34:50,221 --> 00:34:51,456
It's probably spicier now.

954
00:34:51,522 --> 00:34:52,890
-It's spicy now.
-Let's switch seats.

955
00:34:52,957 --> 00:34:54,592
It seems really spicy.

956
00:34:54,659 --> 00:34:56,594
It smells spicy.

957
00:34:56,661 --> 00:34:57,862
THE ATTACK OF THE SPICY SMOKE

958
00:34:58,796 --> 00:35:00,031
It's coming at us.

959
00:35:00,364 --> 00:35:01,365
HOT WATER!

960
00:35:01,432 --> 00:35:03,000
We're smelling the smoke now.

961
00:35:03,634 --> 00:35:05,636
SPREAD, MALA SMOKE

962
00:35:05,703 --> 00:35:07,038
-Whoa.
-That's spicy.

963
00:35:07,105 --> 00:35:10,274
This isn't just regular old smoke!

964
00:35:10,341 --> 00:35:12,577
It looks so good.
I'm so excited right now.

965
00:35:13,845 --> 00:35:15,480
That's all pepper smoke.

966
00:35:15,546 --> 00:35:17,181
-It's stinging my eyes too.
-Mine too.

967
00:35:17,248 --> 00:35:18,516
My eyes sting.

968
00:35:18,583 --> 00:35:20,251
-It stings.
-Like CBRN training.

969
00:35:20,518 --> 00:35:21,752
WHAT ABOUT THE SPICY SISTERS?

970
00:35:21,819 --> 00:35:23,221
It smells so delicious.

971
00:35:23,287 --> 00:35:25,523
-It's a CBRN training.
-It smells so good.

972
00:35:25,590 --> 00:35:27,358
-It smells good, though.
-I love it.

973
00:35:27,425 --> 00:35:28,726
They look so happy.

974
00:35:28,793 --> 00:35:31,596
-It smells so good.
-I'm drooling so much that I can't talk.

975
00:35:31,662 --> 00:35:33,998
The sisters look absolutely excited.

976
00:35:34,065 --> 00:35:35,566
YAY

977
00:35:35,933 --> 00:35:37,201
ADDING MORE R-R-RED FLAVOR

978
00:35:37,468 --> 00:35:39,337
MALA HOT POT SAUCE

979
00:35:39,403 --> 00:35:40,705
That's strong.

980
00:35:40,771 --> 00:35:43,007
-That's strong.
-Yes, that's strong.

981
00:35:43,074 --> 00:35:44,809
That's strong.

982
00:35:44,876 --> 00:35:46,410
You don't even eat spicy food.

983
00:35:46,477 --> 00:35:47,445
I do.

984
00:35:47,512 --> 00:35:48,913
I only eat spicy food.

985
00:35:48,980 --> 00:35:50,848
-Oyster sauce?
-Oyster sauce.

986
00:35:50,915 --> 00:35:52,783
Is that bird's eye chili, Eun-yeong?

987
00:35:52,850 --> 00:35:54,118
Yes, I'm using them.

988
00:35:54,185 --> 00:35:56,387
-She's adding the Vietnamese chili.
-Everything goes in.

989
00:35:56,454 --> 00:35:59,557
She added them whole.

990
00:36:00,224 --> 00:36:01,859
That looks really spicy.

991
00:36:03,127 --> 00:36:04,095
EMERGENCY

992
00:36:04,729 --> 00:36:06,164
That looks like an inferno.

993
00:36:06,898 --> 00:36:07,899
It looks so spicy.

994
00:36:08,065 --> 00:36:10,401
LICKING HER LIPS

995
00:36:10,468 --> 00:36:12,703
You have under ten minutes left.

996
00:36:12,770 --> 00:36:14,372
It smells so good.

997
00:36:14,438 --> 00:36:15,840
What are you grinding, Ho-young?

998
00:36:15,907 --> 00:36:17,308
PEPERONCINO

999
00:36:17,375 --> 00:36:18,643
It looks like peperoncino.

1000
00:36:18,709 --> 00:36:20,978
He now has
Cheongyang peppers in his hands.

1001
00:36:21,045 --> 00:36:22,046
Cheongyang peppers.

1002
00:36:22,113 --> 00:36:24,182
Shouldn't you be
snacking on them as you cook?

1003
00:36:24,248 --> 00:36:25,082
LAUGHING

1004
00:36:25,850 --> 00:36:27,118
WHY NOT?

1005
00:36:27,585 --> 00:36:29,053
CRUNCHY PEPPER

1006
00:36:29,120 --> 00:36:31,022
He's serious about spicy food.

1007
00:36:31,956 --> 00:36:33,658
He even snacks on them as he cooks.

1008
00:36:33,724 --> 00:36:34,559
CRUNCHING

1009
00:36:34,926 --> 00:36:36,160
AND THE BIRD'S EYE CHILI

1010
00:36:36,227 --> 00:36:37,061
Whoa, hey!

1011
00:36:37,128 --> 00:36:38,596
That's going to kill you.

1012
00:36:38,996 --> 00:36:40,798
…WHAT DID I JUST WITNESS?

1013
00:36:40,865 --> 00:36:42,500
Why did you eat the red one?

1014
00:36:42,567 --> 00:36:43,668
COUGH…

1015
00:36:43,834 --> 00:36:45,102
YOU SAID YOU COULD TAKE THE SPICE

1016
00:36:45,570 --> 00:36:47,138
HE MUST'VE GONE WAY OVERBOARD…

1017
00:36:47,772 --> 00:36:49,407
Start whistling.

1018
00:36:49,473 --> 00:36:51,175
-Whistle.
-He's coughing.

1019
00:36:51,809 --> 00:36:53,077
CLEARING THROAT…

1020
00:36:53,644 --> 00:36:54,879
He ground the Cheongyang pepper.

1021
00:36:54,946 --> 00:36:57,448
Look at the amount of chili peppers.
I think he'll add them all.

1022
00:36:57,515 --> 00:37:00,685
-He's grabbing rice.
-He's making rice balls.

1023
00:37:00,751 --> 00:37:01,786
Sea squirt rice balls.

1024
00:37:01,852 --> 00:37:04,789
-Salted pollack roe?
-He adds salted pollack roe.

1025
00:37:04,855 --> 00:37:06,090
That has to be good.

1026
00:37:06,557 --> 00:37:08,292
CHOPPING SOMETHING…

1027
00:37:08,359 --> 00:37:11,062
-What's that?
-<i>Shio kombu, </i>kelp seasoning.

1028
00:37:11,596 --> 00:37:12,530
KELP SEASONING IN!

1029
00:37:12,597 --> 00:37:14,198
Did you add kelp seasoning, Ho-young?

1030
00:37:14,265 --> 00:37:17,001
Yes, I chopped
some kelp seasoning into the rice.

1031
00:37:17,068 --> 00:37:19,437
I love rice balls
made with kelp seasoning.

1032
00:37:19,837 --> 00:37:20,705
WATER PARSLEY

1033
00:37:20,771 --> 00:37:23,007
-He's adding water parsley too.
-That looks so good.

1034
00:37:23,074 --> 00:37:25,910
It's for the aroma and the texture.

1035
00:37:25,977 --> 00:37:27,245
That looks so good.

1036
00:37:27,311 --> 00:37:28,946
I can't take my eyes off both.

1037
00:37:29,547 --> 00:37:31,816
STARTING ON THE WATER-POACHED BEEF

1038
00:37:31,882 --> 00:37:33,684
What's the cut you're using, Eun-yeong?

1039
00:37:33,751 --> 00:37:35,519
-Tenderloin?
-Yes, beef tenderloin.

1040
00:37:35,586 --> 00:37:37,188
She can still answer

1041
00:37:37,255 --> 00:37:39,123
while she's busy cooking.

1042
00:37:39,357 --> 00:37:41,292
EGG

1043
00:37:41,525 --> 00:37:44,028
-Isn't Chef Eun-yeong really fast?
-The way her hands move.

1044
00:37:44,095 --> 00:37:45,529
5. MIX BEEF WITH EGG WASH AND STARCH

1045
00:37:45,596 --> 00:37:49,834
She's marinating beef
to make the water-poached beef.

1046
00:37:49,900 --> 00:37:52,003
-I see.
-It's to make the meat more tender.

1047
00:37:52,069 --> 00:37:54,639
-It goes into the oil.
-It's going into the oil?

1048
00:37:54,705 --> 00:37:56,407
What does that do?

1049
00:37:56,474 --> 00:37:58,476
-It makes the meat tender.
-It becomes very tender.

1050
00:37:58,542 --> 00:38:00,411
BIBI's quite interested in cooking.

1051
00:38:00,478 --> 00:38:02,079
I'll have to try it at home.

1052
00:38:02,146 --> 00:38:04,448
Cooking meat in the oil
will make it even more tender.

1053
00:38:04,515 --> 00:38:06,083
They only have six minutes left.

1054
00:38:06,150 --> 00:38:07,318
-Six minutes.
-Can they finish?

1055
00:38:07,385 --> 00:38:09,654
It's even fiercer in real life
compared to what's shown on TV.

1056
00:38:09,720 --> 00:38:11,589
Of course.
The camera doesn't do them justice.

1057
00:38:11,656 --> 00:38:13,658
It doesn't. It's a war here.

1058
00:38:13,924 --> 00:38:14,792
OPENS THE PRESSURE COOKER

1059
00:38:14,859 --> 00:38:16,227
He opens the cooker.

1060
00:38:16,294 --> 00:38:18,896
Ho-young opens the pressure cooker first.

1061
00:38:18,963 --> 00:38:20,831
Whoa. That's spicy.

1062
00:38:20,898 --> 00:38:23,067
-That's spicy.
-That one's spicy.

1063
00:38:23,434 --> 00:38:25,136
THAT'S MY DIFFUSER

1064
00:38:25,202 --> 00:38:26,537
-It smells good.
-But it smells good.

1065
00:38:27,071 --> 00:38:28,306
COUGHING

1066
00:38:28,673 --> 00:38:30,107
IT'S KILLING NAM-NO…

1067
00:38:30,174 --> 00:38:31,976
Look at Chef Nam-no.

1068
00:38:32,843 --> 00:38:34,178
It's spicy.

1069
00:38:34,578 --> 00:38:38,449
MOVING THE GALBI-JJIM INTO A PAN

1070
00:38:39,450 --> 00:38:41,585
-It's so red.
-It looks spicy.

1071
00:38:41,652 --> 00:38:42,820
Five and a half minutes.

1072
00:38:42,887 --> 00:38:44,922
It's probably spicier
than that famous spicy pork cutlet.

1073
00:38:44,989 --> 00:38:46,424
That'd kill you.

1074
00:38:47,925 --> 00:38:49,994
THESE CAN POTENTIALLY
MAKE THEM SICK AFTERWARD

1075
00:38:50,061 --> 00:38:52,897
They both look so red.
Hey! Wide glass noodles!

1076
00:38:52,963 --> 00:38:55,566
-Wide glass noodles!
-What are those noodles?

1077
00:38:55,633 --> 00:38:56,534
Wide glass noodles!

1078
00:38:56,600 --> 00:38:58,302
Wide glass noodles.

1079
00:38:58,369 --> 00:38:59,603
Young people love them.

1080
00:38:59,670 --> 00:39:02,039
-It's going to be so chewy.
-They must love them.

1081
00:39:02,106 --> 00:39:04,575
Sung-ju, can you go check on the kitchen?

1082
00:39:04,642 --> 00:39:06,644
Well, I'm kind of hesitant.

1083
00:39:06,711 --> 00:39:09,680
Please find out if those dishes
can help the sisters make up.

1084
00:39:09,747 --> 00:39:11,349
They look way too spicy.

1085
00:39:11,415 --> 00:39:14,285
-Please go check.
-What do I do?

1086
00:39:14,352 --> 00:39:15,653
Time to go!

1087
00:39:15,720 --> 00:39:17,755
STALKED SEA SQUIRT THAT "SOMEONE" PREPPED

1088
00:39:18,155 --> 00:39:19,090
3. MIX SEA SQUIRT WITH RICE

1089
00:39:19,156 --> 00:39:21,392
Eun-yeong and Ho-young's
spicy dish cook-off.

1090
00:39:21,459 --> 00:39:23,794
This one looks insane.

1091
00:39:23,861 --> 00:39:24,929
-Sung-ju.
-Yes?

1092
00:39:24,995 --> 00:39:26,397
Try the <i>mala </i>soup,

1093
00:39:26,464 --> 00:39:27,765
the one Eun-yeong made.

1094
00:39:27,832 --> 00:39:30,634
-I'm too scared.
-But you still have to do it.

1095
00:39:30,701 --> 00:39:31,802
It looks so scary.

1096
00:39:31,869 --> 00:39:33,771
Let me try just a sip.

1097
00:39:33,838 --> 00:39:34,939
No way. It's too spicy.

1098
00:39:35,005 --> 00:39:36,707
-It's probably very hot.
-I'll try it.

1099
00:39:37,441 --> 00:39:38,642
ARRRRGH!

1100
00:39:38,709 --> 00:39:39,844
What's it like?

1101
00:39:39,910 --> 00:39:41,479
My tongue feels numb.

1102
00:39:41,545 --> 00:39:43,013
That sounds great.

1103
00:39:43,080 --> 00:39:44,482
It numbs your tongue.

1104
00:39:44,548 --> 00:39:46,484
I can't eat the leftovers today.

1105
00:39:46,550 --> 00:39:47,485
My eyes are burning.

1106
00:39:47,918 --> 00:39:49,019
HO-YOUNG WON'T BACK DOWN!

1107
00:39:49,086 --> 00:39:50,554
He added lots of ground pepper too.

1108
00:39:51,255 --> 00:39:52,390
PEPERONCINO

1109
00:39:52,456 --> 00:39:54,125
-No, stop adding so much.
-Those?

1110
00:39:54,191 --> 00:39:56,927
He just stashed peperoncino in.

1111
00:39:57,995 --> 00:39:59,697
He added more chili peppers!

1112
00:40:00,564 --> 00:40:02,533
SPICY LEVEL GOES UP

1113
00:40:02,600 --> 00:40:05,236
-Try that one too.
-Red, red, Ho-young.

1114
00:40:07,438 --> 00:40:08,272
THE DEATH OF THE "RED RED" TREND

1115
00:40:08,339 --> 00:40:10,207
It looks red. Very red.

1116
00:40:10,274 --> 00:40:11,675
He's adorable.

1117
00:40:11,742 --> 00:40:15,813
-If you don't feel it, it's not too spicy.
-Right. If I do, it's spicier.

1118
00:40:16,647 --> 00:40:18,749
Does it taste red red?

1119
00:40:18,816 --> 00:40:20,684
IS THE GALBI-JJIM TOTALLY RED RED?

1120
00:40:21,752 --> 00:40:23,988
-You don't feel it?
-I guess it's not too spicy.

1121
00:40:24,054 --> 00:40:26,290
-Huh?
-He doesn't feel it.

1122
00:40:26,357 --> 00:40:28,025
There's a difference.

1123
00:40:28,092 --> 00:40:29,360
A different kind of spiciness?

1124
00:40:29,427 --> 00:40:32,163
This was spicy
from the moment it hit my mouth,

1125
00:40:32,229 --> 00:40:34,265
-but this one hits you later.
-Later?

1126
00:40:35,299 --> 00:40:37,435
I think I can handle this one.

1127
00:40:37,501 --> 00:40:38,836
ADDING STALKED SEA SQUIRT

1128
00:40:39,804 --> 00:40:40,938
Whoa, what was that?

1129
00:40:41,972 --> 00:40:43,441
RAN AWAY QUICKLY

1130
00:40:43,507 --> 00:40:45,042
-Warn us beforehand!
-Sorry about that.

1131
00:40:45,109 --> 00:40:46,977
She's saying you're talking too much.

1132
00:40:47,044 --> 00:40:49,547
HO-YOUNG'S DISH ISN'T AS SPICY

1133
00:40:49,613 --> 00:40:51,582
You said you liked spicy food,

1134
00:40:51,649 --> 00:40:53,451
but it wasn't even spicy enough for me.

1135
00:40:53,684 --> 00:40:55,386
EUN-YEONG WILL WIN, GREEN GREEN

1136
00:40:55,453 --> 00:40:57,721
The meat is ready.

1137
00:40:57,788 --> 00:41:00,324
It looks so good. Is that meat?

1138
00:41:00,391 --> 00:41:03,027
The cabbage will
probably reduce the spiciness.

1139
00:41:03,093 --> 00:41:04,662
Two minutes and twelve seconds.

1140
00:41:04,728 --> 00:41:07,431
-You haven't opened the pressure cooker.
-No.

1141
00:41:07,498 --> 00:41:09,133
Ho-young makes rice balls.

1142
00:41:10,067 --> 00:41:11,235
They look so pretty.

1143
00:41:11,302 --> 00:41:12,870
Look at the rice balls.

1144
00:41:12,937 --> 00:41:14,772
Those rice balls are so cute.

1145
00:41:15,906 --> 00:41:17,508
A LOAD OF SESAME OIL

1146
00:41:17,708 --> 00:41:23,347
7. MIX OIL WITH CRUSHED GARLIC,
VINEGAR, SUGAR, AND CHILI FLAKES

1147
00:41:24,048 --> 00:41:25,749
MAKING DIPS!

1148
00:41:26,650 --> 00:41:27,785
What now? I have to add this.

1149
00:41:27,852 --> 00:41:30,855
-There's still some pressure inside.
-It's not fully released.

1150
00:41:31,522 --> 00:41:33,357
GOING FOR A COLD SHOWER!

1151
00:41:33,424 --> 00:41:34,758
It's an emergency measure.

1152
00:41:34,825 --> 00:41:36,727
-Is this the side dish?
-Yes, with the pork skin.

1153
00:41:36,794 --> 00:41:38,128
I'll mix it with the pork skin.

1154
00:41:38,195 --> 00:41:39,997
The lid is off!

1155
00:41:40,064 --> 00:41:42,666
Eun-yeong managed to get the lid off.

1156
00:41:42,733 --> 00:41:44,335
Hurry up and mix them.

1157
00:41:44,401 --> 00:41:45,936
-Move quickly.
-It's still hot!

1158
00:41:46,003 --> 00:41:48,172
It's probably still hot.

1159
00:41:48,239 --> 00:41:49,907
She's mixing it with the veggies.

1160
00:41:49,974 --> 00:41:51,375
A minute and a half.

1161
00:41:51,442 --> 00:41:53,310
-Not much time left.
-Can they do this?

1162
00:41:53,377 --> 00:41:55,112
-What?
-Is this possible?

1163
00:41:55,179 --> 00:41:57,114
You don't have much time.
A minute and a half.

1164
00:41:57,181 --> 00:41:59,884
-Whoa. All that?
-He's adding more Cheongyang pepper.

1165
00:41:59,950 --> 00:42:01,252
You have about a minute now.

1166
00:42:01,318 --> 00:42:02,887
INTO A LARGE METAL BOWL

1167
00:42:02,953 --> 00:42:04,421
You don't have much time left.

1168
00:42:05,356 --> 00:42:06,657
The metal bowl.

1169
00:42:06,724 --> 00:42:09,026
That's spicy <i>galbi-jjim, </i>Daegu-style.

1170
00:42:09,093 --> 00:42:11,795
-I really love that.
-He's using a metal bowl.

1171
00:42:11,862 --> 00:42:13,163
It's in a metal bowl.

1172
00:42:13,230 --> 00:42:15,466
-Keep it up, Ho-young.
-That's insane.

1173
00:42:15,533 --> 00:42:17,768
You're doing great. It looks so good.

1174
00:42:17,835 --> 00:42:19,837
-Forty seconds.
-Spicy <i>galbi-jjim</i>.

1175
00:42:19,904 --> 00:42:21,572
She's using wide glass noodles too.

1176
00:42:21,639 --> 00:42:23,841
We really love glass noodles.

1177
00:42:23,908 --> 00:42:25,910
She's serving it in an earthen pot.

1178
00:42:26,644 --> 00:42:28,979
SHE'S USING AN EARTHEN POT

1179
00:42:29,046 --> 00:42:30,681
He's adding more?

1180
00:42:30,748 --> 00:42:32,283
ADD MORE PEPERONCINO

1181
00:42:32,349 --> 00:42:33,584
What about green onion kimchi?

1182
00:42:33,651 --> 00:42:35,819
-Yes, the green onion.
-The green onion kimchi.

1183
00:42:36,253 --> 00:42:38,022
THE CHERRY ON TOP, GREEN ONION KIMCHI

1184
00:42:38,322 --> 00:42:39,790
IT LOOKS INSANELY DELICIOUS

1185
00:42:39,857 --> 00:42:41,692
You would've forgotten
if I hadn't mentioned it.

1186
00:42:41,759 --> 00:42:44,128
-It would've been bad.
-Green onion kimchi.

1187
00:42:44,194 --> 00:42:45,896
Twenty-five seconds.

1188
00:42:45,963 --> 00:42:47,364
GREEN ONION KIMCHI, GALBI-JJIM, RED RED
IS DONE

1189
00:42:47,431 --> 00:42:48,299
He's done?

1190
00:42:48,365 --> 00:42:50,467
So this is how you make
the pork skin dish.

1191
00:42:50,701 --> 00:42:52,670
SESAME OIL, VINEGAR, SOY SAUCE

1192
00:42:52,736 --> 00:42:54,038
-Keep it up.
-It's a side dish.

1193
00:42:54,104 --> 00:42:55,673
-Fifteen seconds.
-Fifteen seconds.

1194
00:42:55,739 --> 00:42:57,841
Once Eun-yeong finishes, that's it.

1195
00:42:57,908 --> 00:42:59,410
-Five.
-Five.

1196
00:42:59,476 --> 00:43:00,578
-Four.
-Four.

1197
00:43:00,644 --> 00:43:02,146
-Three. Two.
-Three. Two.

1198
00:43:02,212 --> 00:43:03,781
-One.
-One.

1199
00:43:03,847 --> 00:43:05,282
-The end.
-Time's up.

1200
00:43:05,349 --> 00:43:08,586
Eun-yeong finishes too.
Both chefs are done cooking.

1201
00:43:08,919 --> 00:43:10,487
THE RED-FLAVORED DISHES ARE FINISHED

1202
00:43:11,689 --> 00:43:13,691
-They really did it in 15 minutes.
-They did it in 15 minutes.

1203
00:43:13,757 --> 00:43:15,025
Did you think we lied?

1204
00:43:15,092 --> 00:43:17,227
-I mean, still.
-I thought you'd have a leeway.

1205
00:43:17,294 --> 00:43:18,862
No, when the time's up, it's done.

1206
00:43:18,929 --> 00:43:20,831
-Whoa.
-Whoa.

1207
00:43:20,898 --> 00:43:22,800
That was too shocking.

1208
00:43:22,866 --> 00:43:23,734
It was.

1209
00:43:23,801 --> 00:43:25,202
Here they come.

1210
00:43:25,269 --> 00:43:27,738
THE FIERY MAKEUP DISHES ARE ON THEIR WAY

1211
00:43:28,205 --> 00:43:29,373
WOW

1212
00:43:29,440 --> 00:43:30,274
It smells so good.

1213
00:43:30,341 --> 00:43:32,977
-This <i>mala </i>diffuser.
-Diffuser.

1214
00:43:33,043 --> 00:43:37,681
Both chefs' dishes are finished
for BIBI and NaKyoung.

1215
00:43:38,048 --> 00:43:39,783
YAY

1216
00:43:39,850 --> 00:43:42,319
As a fan who has watched us
since our first episode,

1217
00:43:42,386 --> 00:43:43,887
what was it like to see them cook?

1218
00:43:43,954 --> 00:43:45,489
Despite the rules, I thought…

1219
00:43:45,556 --> 00:43:46,557
I thought you'd give

1220
00:43:46,624 --> 00:43:48,392
-a little more time.
-Yes.

1221
00:43:48,459 --> 00:43:49,760
But nothing at all.

1222
00:43:49,827 --> 00:43:51,595
It was 15 minutes and nothing more.

1223
00:43:51,662 --> 00:43:53,831
-Seriously.
-Yes, I was surprised to see it.

1224
00:43:53,897 --> 00:43:56,667
-I thought they could go, "Wait, timeout."
-No way.

1225
00:43:56,734 --> 00:43:58,535
Taking time off.

1226
00:43:58,602 --> 00:44:00,437
Things happen as you cook.

1227
00:44:00,504 --> 00:44:02,039
We're only humans.

1228
00:44:02,106 --> 00:44:03,007
True.

1229
00:44:03,073 --> 00:44:04,908
-At times, you can make a mistake.
-Yes.

1230
00:44:04,975 --> 00:44:07,444
Even if you threw in three more minutes,
you'd let it go

1231
00:44:07,511 --> 00:44:08,912
with the good old Korean love.

1232
00:44:09,880 --> 00:44:12,049
But they had to stop after 15 minutes.

1233
00:44:12,116 --> 00:44:13,150
So cutthroat.

1234
00:44:13,217 --> 00:44:15,119
-It's so transparent.
-Yes.

1235
00:44:15,185 --> 00:44:18,622
Ho-young was the first to finish
making those dishes for the sisters.

1236
00:44:18,689 --> 00:44:20,624
TRYING HO-YOUNG'S DISH FIRST

1237
00:44:20,691 --> 00:44:22,026
Please try that first.

1238
00:44:24,294 --> 00:44:27,765
RED RED GREEN ONION KIMCHI AND GALBI-JJIM

1239
00:44:28,699 --> 00:44:31,902
SALTY AND BRINY SEA SQUIRT RICE BALLS

1240
00:44:32,803 --> 00:44:36,807
CHEF JUNG HO-YOUNG'S
GREEN ONION KIMCHI, GALBI-JJIM, RED RED

1241
00:44:36,874 --> 00:44:39,009
-Thank you.
-Here's the thing.

1242
00:44:39,076 --> 00:44:42,346
-It's still hot. That's why.
-You can see the steam.

1243
00:44:42,413 --> 00:44:44,381
-How should they eat this?
-First of all,

1244
00:44:44,448 --> 00:44:47,484
wrap a piece of meat
with green onion kimchi

1245
00:44:47,551 --> 00:44:49,620
drenched in the soup.

1246
00:44:49,687 --> 00:44:51,121
-Wrap it around.
-Usually…

1247
00:44:51,188 --> 00:44:52,823
-I got it.
-…guests wait

1248
00:44:52,890 --> 00:44:55,693
until the chefs tell them
how to eat their dishes.

1249
00:44:55,759 --> 00:44:58,462
-Not them.
-They started serving it first.

1250
00:44:58,529 --> 00:45:02,099
-Have that first, and then the rice.
-And then the rice.

1251
00:45:02,166 --> 00:45:03,834
-I see.
-The spicy dish first.

1252
00:45:03,901 --> 00:45:06,336
She's serving it to her sister first.

1253
00:45:06,403 --> 00:45:07,337
She gave it to NaKyoung.

1254
00:45:07,404 --> 00:45:08,338
-How sweet.
-So nice.

1255
00:45:08,405 --> 00:45:10,874
-How sweet.
-She's here to make peace with her.

1256
00:45:10,941 --> 00:45:12,409
-How nice.
-Are you trying to make peace?

1257
00:45:12,476 --> 00:45:13,677
THE SPICY SISTERS ARE MAKING UP

1258
00:45:13,744 --> 00:45:16,513
They remind me of my sister and me.

1259
00:45:16,580 --> 00:45:19,416
Wait, he added
wide glass noodles too, NaKyoung.

1260
00:45:19,483 --> 00:45:21,952
-I'm drooling so much.
-It looks spicy.

1261
00:45:22,019 --> 00:45:23,087
Wide glass noodles.

1262
00:45:23,153 --> 00:45:24,988
-Take a good look.
-I feel…

1263
00:45:25,055 --> 00:45:27,758
-I feel so rushed.
-She might take the bigger piece.

1264
00:45:29,193 --> 00:45:31,462
-Take off a few pieces.
-And telling her,

1265
00:45:31,528 --> 00:45:32,996
"That's the delicious part."

1266
00:45:33,964 --> 00:45:36,934
-It's the delicious part.
-Are you sure it's good?

1267
00:45:37,000 --> 00:45:38,736
You've grabbed lots of meaty pieces.

1268
00:45:40,170 --> 00:45:42,673
-This is punishment for us.
-It looks spicy.

1269
00:45:42,740 --> 00:45:45,709
-Thank you for the food.
-Now, time to try it.

1270
00:45:45,776 --> 00:45:46,844
Wow.

1271
00:45:47,911 --> 00:45:49,513
TOPPING TENDERLOIN

1272
00:45:49,580 --> 00:45:51,849
WITH A PIECE OF GREEN ONION KIMCHI

1273
00:45:51,915 --> 00:45:54,818
That's it.
Wrap a piece of meat with kimchi.

1274
00:45:54,885 --> 00:45:57,554
TAKING A BITE TOGETHER

1275
00:45:58,222 --> 00:46:00,190
Red, red.

1276
00:46:00,257 --> 00:46:02,059
LOVES HIS JOKES♥

1277
00:46:02,626 --> 00:46:03,527
Red, red.

1278
00:46:03,594 --> 00:46:07,264
I wonder how spicy it is for them.
Look at BIBI go.

1279
00:46:09,133 --> 00:46:10,868
-It's good.
-It is?

1280
00:46:10,934 --> 00:46:12,870
There is this

1281
00:46:12,936 --> 00:46:14,772
-odd balance.
-I see.

1282
00:46:14,838 --> 00:46:17,374
What if he forgot
to add the green onion kimchi?

1283
00:46:17,441 --> 00:46:19,209
-It's sweet, salty, and spicy.
-Yes.

1284
00:46:19,276 --> 00:46:21,678
It's not just spicy. It's sweet too.

1285
00:46:21,745 --> 00:46:24,148
But it's not overly sweet.

1286
00:46:24,214 --> 00:46:25,315
It just makes you go,

1287
00:46:25,382 --> 00:46:26,316
"It's spicy."

1288
00:46:28,485 --> 00:46:30,053
"It's sweet too." Like that.

1289
00:46:30,120 --> 00:46:32,322
-It's so hard to watch.
-It's so good.

1290
00:46:32,389 --> 00:46:34,424
It feels so comforting.

1291
00:46:34,491 --> 00:46:35,325
A soul food.

1292
00:46:35,392 --> 00:46:37,528
-I see.
-It's worthy of making peace.

1293
00:46:38,028 --> 00:46:40,063
ANOTHER BIG BITE

1294
00:46:40,130 --> 00:46:43,467
-Spicy <i>galbi-jjim </i>comes with sweetness.
-I see.

1295
00:46:43,534 --> 00:46:45,569
-It's not as good if it's just spicy.
-I need rice.

1296
00:46:45,636 --> 00:46:47,938
He made good use
of the green onion kimchi.

1297
00:46:48,005 --> 00:46:48,972
Go for the rice balls.

1298
00:46:49,039 --> 00:46:50,207
YOU'RE RIGHT

1299
00:46:50,274 --> 00:46:52,643
-Go for it when you crave rice.
-That's also spicy too.

1300
00:46:53,644 --> 00:46:55,512
-Isn't it spicy?
-It must be.

1301
00:46:55,579 --> 00:46:57,614
-Patricia.
-Yes?

1302
00:46:57,681 --> 00:46:58,615
Wipe your mouth.

1303
00:46:59,416 --> 00:47:02,219
-My gosh.
-Your mouth was open too wide.

1304
00:47:02,286 --> 00:47:04,154
HER MOUTH IS FLOODING

1305
00:47:04,221 --> 00:47:06,123
Can you wipe the table too?

1306
00:47:08,292 --> 00:47:09,560
They both eat so well.

1307
00:47:09,626 --> 00:47:10,727
Sea squirt rice balls.

1308
00:47:12,696 --> 00:47:15,232
It smells like stalked sea squirt.

1309
00:47:15,299 --> 00:47:16,366
That's new.

1310
00:47:16,433 --> 00:47:18,202
I'm not really into stalked sea squirt,

1311
00:47:18,268 --> 00:47:21,705
-but this is somewhat…
-She said NaKyoung liked it.

1312
00:47:21,772 --> 00:47:23,941
<i>-I added some pollack roe.</i>
<i>-Yes, salted pollack roe.</i>

1313
00:47:24,007 --> 00:47:26,710
<i>-Then added stalked sea squirt too.</i>
<i>-They go so well.</i>

1314
00:47:26,777 --> 00:47:28,412
It has a pleasant briny smell.

1315
00:47:28,478 --> 00:47:30,747
-A pleasant briny smell.
-Subtle.

1316
00:47:30,814 --> 00:47:32,015
What about the spiciness?

1317
00:47:32,082 --> 00:47:34,218
I can take something even spicier,

1318
00:47:34,284 --> 00:47:37,855
-but this is something I can enjoy.
-Something close to normal.

1319
00:47:37,921 --> 00:47:40,190
-Something that normal humans eat.
-It's humanely tolerable.

1320
00:47:40,257 --> 00:47:43,427
-"Humanely tolerable"?
-"Humanely tolerable"?

1321
00:47:43,493 --> 00:47:44,328
CRUNCH

1322
00:47:45,028 --> 00:47:47,564
-Did you add stalked sea squirt to this?
-I did.

1323
00:47:47,631 --> 00:47:49,366
I can hear the crunchy sound.

1324
00:47:49,433 --> 00:47:51,501
-It sounds so delicious.
-It's so good.

1325
00:47:51,568 --> 00:47:52,870
Does the kimchi play a part?

1326
00:47:52,936 --> 00:47:54,972
-It's so important.
-It is?

1327
00:47:55,038 --> 00:47:58,375
<i>Its pungent flavor is the kick.</i>

1328
00:47:58,809 --> 00:48:00,878
THE SPICY SISTERS WON'T STOP

1329
00:48:00,944 --> 00:48:02,946
They're tiny, yet they eat so well.

1330
00:48:03,513 --> 00:48:04,982
They don't even stop for a second.

1331
00:48:05,048 --> 00:48:07,451
-And she eats it in one bite.
-She eats well.

1332
00:48:07,684 --> 00:48:09,119
JEALOUS…

1333
00:48:09,386 --> 00:48:11,321
DROOLING

1334
00:48:11,388 --> 00:48:13,457
This is almost torture.

1335
00:48:13,523 --> 00:48:14,625
-What's wrong?
-What is it?

1336
00:48:14,691 --> 00:48:16,827
I thought you were here to give us food.

1337
00:48:18,362 --> 00:48:20,264
-I thought he came to give it to us.
-Really.

1338
00:48:20,330 --> 00:48:23,767
-Water-poached beef is turning into jerky.
-Right.

1339
00:48:23,834 --> 00:48:26,303
-Eun-yeong is up next.
-Eun-yeong's dish remains.

1340
00:48:26,370 --> 00:48:27,638
-You have to eat that too.
-You must.

1341
00:48:27,704 --> 00:48:30,274
-We can eat it all.
-You can eat more later.

1342
00:48:30,340 --> 00:48:31,441
All right.

1343
00:48:31,508 --> 00:48:32,609
FEELING PROUD

1344
00:48:33,243 --> 00:48:36,513
NEXT, EUN-YEONG'S DISH

1345
00:48:36,914 --> 00:48:40,751
WATER-POACHED BEEF WITH A DEEP RED FLAVOR

1346
00:48:41,551 --> 00:48:44,187
SESAME OIL SAUCE
TO BRING OUT THE BEEFY FLAVOR

1347
00:48:45,355 --> 00:48:48,258
PORK SKIN SALAD WITH LOTS OF WATER PARSLEY

1348
00:48:49,459 --> 00:48:52,529
CHEF PARK EUN-YEONG'S
RED FLAVOR, CURIOUS, MALA

1349
00:48:52,596 --> 00:48:54,731
You can dip the beef

1350
00:48:54,798 --> 00:48:56,033
in the sauce in front of you.

1351
00:48:56,099 --> 00:48:57,968
When people go to hot pot places,

1352
00:48:58,035 --> 00:49:01,371
many Koreans like to dip the food
into peanut or sesame sauce.

1353
00:49:01,438 --> 00:49:06,376
But in Sichuan, it's sesame oil,
vinegar, salt, and garlic.

1354
00:49:06,443 --> 00:49:07,978
-I see.
-Whoa.

1355
00:49:08,045 --> 00:49:10,314
-Thank you.
-Thank you.

1356
00:49:10,380 --> 00:49:13,884
What's this red flavor like?

1357
00:49:14,685 --> 00:49:17,321
It was different
from Ho-young's spiciness.

1358
00:49:17,387 --> 00:49:21,191
-It was something Gen MZ would love.
-This one.

1359
00:49:21,258 --> 00:49:23,994
This is Chinese spiciness,
and Ho-young's is Korean spiciness.

1360
00:49:24,061 --> 00:49:25,862
-Yes, that's the difference.
-Yes.

1361
00:49:25,929 --> 00:49:28,298
-Was it much spicier?
-It must be spicier.

1362
00:49:28,365 --> 00:49:30,534
Why are you looking at her? What is it?

1363
00:49:30,600 --> 00:49:31,868
-It must be spicy.
-What is it, BIBI?

1364
00:49:31,935 --> 00:49:33,603
-It's spicy.
-It is?

1365
00:49:33,670 --> 00:49:37,040
-But it's so good. It's aromatic.
-Aromatic?

1366
00:49:37,107 --> 00:49:39,176
It smells so good.

1367
00:49:40,043 --> 00:49:42,412
A BITE OF THE GLASS NOODLE

1368
00:49:42,879 --> 00:49:44,281
SLURP

1369
00:49:44,781 --> 00:49:46,283
EXHILARATED

1370
00:49:46,350 --> 00:49:47,184
-That's it.
-She likes it.

1371
00:49:47,250 --> 00:49:50,687
-It must be good.
-It has to be good.

1372
00:49:50,754 --> 00:49:53,590
We call this Jang Won-young glass noodles.

1373
00:49:53,657 --> 00:49:55,625
-Have you heard of it?
-It's hot.

1374
00:49:55,692 --> 00:49:58,261
It's different
from the glass noodles we eat.

1375
00:49:58,328 --> 00:49:59,396
What's Jang Won-young glass noodles?

1376
00:49:59,463 --> 00:50:01,465
-Something that Won-young likes?
-Is it spicy?

1377
00:50:01,531 --> 00:50:03,000
But I like those too.

1378
00:50:03,200 --> 00:50:04,034
HAHA

1379
00:50:04,101 --> 00:50:06,236
-Kim Poong glass noodles.
-I like them too.

1380
00:50:06,303 --> 00:50:08,171
Why do you call it that?

1381
00:50:08,238 --> 00:50:10,273
It's a neologism, I think.

1382
00:50:10,340 --> 00:50:11,174
-What?
-It's like

1383
00:50:11,241 --> 00:50:13,810
the opposite of <i>bamti</i>, "tacky,"
Jang Won-young.

1384
00:50:13,877 --> 00:50:17,280
-It's the opposite of <i>bamti?</i>
-It's the antonym of <i>bamti?</i>

1385
00:50:17,347 --> 00:50:19,716
When something's really great,
like some shoes you love,

1386
00:50:19,783 --> 00:50:21,718
you call them Jang Won-young shoes.

1387
00:50:21,785 --> 00:50:23,453
Something that's great.

1388
00:50:23,520 --> 00:50:24,521
I'll start using that.

1389
00:50:26,323 --> 00:50:28,492
What's <i>bamti?</i>
Is it chestnut tiramisu, <i>bam </i>tiramisu?

1390
00:50:28,692 --> 00:50:29,926
LOL

1391
00:50:29,993 --> 00:50:32,062
People use that word
to call stuff that's awful.

1392
00:50:32,129 --> 00:50:33,163
Something that isn't great.

1393
00:50:33,230 --> 00:50:34,731
BAMTI: NEOLOGISM FOR UGLY, TACKY, LOUSY

1394
00:50:34,798 --> 00:50:37,434
-I thought it was <i>bam </i>tiramisu.
-No.

1395
00:50:38,335 --> 00:50:40,370
TOP IT WITH SESAME OIL SAUCE

1396
00:50:40,637 --> 00:50:41,972
YUM

1397
00:50:43,473 --> 00:50:44,574
Mmm.

1398
00:50:44,641 --> 00:50:45,609
Eun-yeong.

1399
00:50:46,643 --> 00:50:49,746
-Do you like the sauce?
-It hits you

1400
00:50:49,813 --> 00:50:52,716
just as the dish
is about to get almost too spicy.

1401
00:50:52,783 --> 00:50:56,953
It comes in and covers your mouth
with sourness and nuttiness.

1402
00:50:57,020 --> 00:50:59,022
-So it plays a big part?
-Yes.

1403
00:50:59,589 --> 00:51:01,091
I'm sorry, but I'm craving a drink.

1404
00:51:01,158 --> 00:51:03,794
-You're craving a drink?
-It calls for a drink.

1405
00:51:03,860 --> 00:51:04,694
It calls for a drink?

1406
00:51:04,761 --> 00:51:08,098
There are different levels
of spiciness for <i>malatang, </i>right?

1407
00:51:08,165 --> 00:51:10,500
From level one to even four or five.

1408
00:51:10,567 --> 00:51:12,269
This has gone way over level five.

1409
00:51:12,335 --> 00:51:13,904
-I see.
-It's even spicier?

1410
00:51:13,970 --> 00:51:15,906
-It's about level seven.
-Really?

1411
00:51:15,972 --> 00:51:17,374
It must be really spicy.

1412
00:51:17,441 --> 00:51:19,476
-We're okay with it.
-It's great.

1413
00:51:19,543 --> 00:51:22,679
It looks like you're sweating a little.

1414
00:51:22,746 --> 00:51:26,283
But after eating something this spicy
and sweating it out,

1415
00:51:26,349 --> 00:51:29,252
-you'd forget about the fight.
-I would forget.

1416
00:51:29,319 --> 00:51:31,888
WHY THE CHOPSTICKS?

1417
00:51:31,955 --> 00:51:34,524
Excuse me. Please do not eat the metal.

1418
00:51:34,591 --> 00:51:35,892
OH MY MY

1419
00:51:35,959 --> 00:51:37,527
-Don't eat it.
-Why did you pick them up?

1420
00:51:37,594 --> 00:51:39,563
-Calm down.
-You're going to love this.

1421
00:51:40,931 --> 00:51:42,299
They don't even care.

1422
00:51:42,632 --> 00:51:44,134
A BITE OF PORK SKIN SALAD

1423
00:51:44,434 --> 00:51:45,836
RESPONDS IMMEDIATELY

1424
00:51:46,136 --> 00:51:47,737
THIS IS INSANE!

1425
00:51:47,971 --> 00:51:49,172
LOVE THE SALAD

1426
00:51:49,239 --> 00:51:50,974
There's a big frenzy over here.

1427
00:51:51,041 --> 00:51:53,510
-The pork skin.
-They're going crazy over the pork skin.

1428
00:51:53,577 --> 00:51:54,444
Who made this?

1429
00:51:55,011 --> 00:51:56,446
Seriously. It's so good.

1430
00:51:56,513 --> 00:51:58,849
Tell them what you meant by
"Who made this?"

1431
00:51:58,915 --> 00:52:00,383
What's that?

1432
00:52:00,450 --> 00:52:02,619
-What's "pomeki?"
-It's "who made this."

1433
00:52:02,686 --> 00:52:04,054
When you eat something really good,

1434
00:52:04,121 --> 00:52:05,422
you get mad and go, "Who made this?"

1435
00:52:05,489 --> 00:52:07,757
-"Who made this?"
-Because it was so good.

1436
00:52:07,824 --> 00:52:09,126
Who made this?

1437
00:52:09,192 --> 00:52:11,428
-Who made this?
-Who made this?

1438
00:52:11,495 --> 00:52:14,731
Then the chef will feel alarmed.
"Is there something wrong?"

1439
00:52:14,798 --> 00:52:16,199
"It's so good!"

1440
00:52:16,266 --> 00:52:17,100
"It's dang good!"

1441
00:52:17,167 --> 00:52:20,570
All these Gen MZ know what it is.

1442
00:52:20,637 --> 00:52:22,439
Who made this?

1443
00:52:23,240 --> 00:52:24,741
-So good!
-So good!

1444
00:52:25,041 --> 00:52:26,309
HAVE WE PUT ANOTHER MEME TO REST?

1445
00:52:26,376 --> 00:52:28,345
They're going crazy over this.

1446
00:52:28,411 --> 00:52:29,713
EATING UP THE SALAD

1447
00:52:29,779 --> 00:52:31,348
They're crazy about the pork skin.

1448
00:52:31,414 --> 00:52:32,849
I don't like anything that tastes sour.

1449
00:52:32,916 --> 00:52:34,217
-I see.
-Yes?

1450
00:52:34,284 --> 00:52:37,888
But it's just sour enough
to feel pleasant.

1451
00:52:37,954 --> 00:52:40,824
It tastes so pleasant.
It smells and feels great too.

1452
00:52:40,891 --> 00:52:42,792
What is this? Prickly ash?

1453
00:52:42,859 --> 00:52:45,462
You had this <i>mala</i>-flavored oil.

1454
00:52:45,529 --> 00:52:48,565
<i>So I mixed that with vinegar,</i>
<i>sugar, and soy sauce.</i>

1455
00:52:48,632 --> 00:52:51,668
<i>-She's good at this.</i>
<i>-And water parsley.</i>

1456
00:52:51,735 --> 00:52:53,136
Is the pork skin cooked well?

1457
00:52:53,703 --> 00:52:55,939
-It's tender.
-And chewy.

1458
00:52:56,006 --> 00:52:57,440
You've cooked it well.

1459
00:52:57,707 --> 00:52:59,342
THE SIBLINGS CONTINUE TO DROOL…

1460
00:52:59,409 --> 00:53:00,277
Want some?

1461
00:53:00,343 --> 00:53:02,746
-Yes!
-Please, if we may.

1462
00:53:02,812 --> 00:53:03,647
Do you want some?

1463
00:53:03,713 --> 00:53:04,915
-Yes.
-We want some!

1464
00:53:04,981 --> 00:53:07,450
-Then scream!
-Yes!

1465
00:53:08,018 --> 00:53:08,885
Give us some!

1466
00:53:09,219 --> 00:53:10,787
APPROVED

1467
00:53:10,854 --> 00:53:11,721
"Give us some!"

1468
00:53:11,788 --> 00:53:13,590
-Give us some!
-Give them some.

1469
00:53:13,657 --> 00:53:16,126
This is Chef Ho-young's dish.

1470
00:53:16,193 --> 00:53:19,296
-Try the meat with kimchi.
-The meat.

1471
00:53:19,362 --> 00:53:20,597
-Whoa.
-I'll try some.

1472
00:53:21,298 --> 00:53:23,366
THE SPICINESS MADE HIM SUDDENLY AGE

1473
00:53:23,433 --> 00:53:25,035
-It's spicy.
-With kimchi.

1474
00:53:25,535 --> 00:53:27,938
PATRICIA TAKES A SPICY BITE TOO

1475
00:53:28,205 --> 00:53:31,007
-Mmm.
-I can't do this.

1476
00:53:31,074 --> 00:53:33,510
It's good. It's so good.

1477
00:53:33,577 --> 00:53:35,512
-Isn't it good?
-Is this okay?

1478
00:53:35,579 --> 00:53:37,480
EVERYTHING AT ONCE

1479
00:53:37,981 --> 00:53:39,716
IN ONE BITE

1480
00:53:40,383 --> 00:53:42,052
-Isn't it spicy?
-No, not at all.

1481
00:53:42,118 --> 00:53:43,420
Not at all?

1482
00:53:43,486 --> 00:53:44,921
BREAKING OUT IN A SWEAT

1483
00:53:45,488 --> 00:53:47,991
JONATHAN BEGINS TO DEVOUR IT TOO

1484
00:53:49,926 --> 00:53:51,628
IMMEDIATELY POPS A RICE BALL IN

1485
00:53:53,396 --> 00:53:54,864
-The pork tastes great.
-It's spicy.

1486
00:53:54,931 --> 00:53:57,834
I've never seen Nam-no
having trouble with food.

1487
00:53:57,901 --> 00:54:00,237
-I'm not good with spicy food.
-Oh, no.

1488
00:54:00,303 --> 00:54:01,771
It's really good,

1489
00:54:01,838 --> 00:54:03,406
-but my tongue can't handle it.
-It can't?

1490
00:54:03,473 --> 00:54:04,608
SAM'S SOUL HAS LEFT HIM TOO

1491
00:54:04,674 --> 00:54:06,776
It's a shame
that you can't eat something this good.

1492
00:54:06,843 --> 00:54:08,511
Man, I give up.

1493
00:54:08,578 --> 00:54:09,646
You can see the effect.

1494
00:54:09,713 --> 00:54:12,983
But this rice ball? Who made this?

1495
00:54:13,049 --> 00:54:14,351
-It's so good.
-Yes, who made this?

1496
00:54:14,417 --> 00:54:17,454
Nathan, you can never eat anything fishy.

1497
00:54:17,520 --> 00:54:19,689
I'm not big on seafood,

1498
00:54:19,756 --> 00:54:23,493
-but it doesn't taste fishy at all.
-You don't even eat sea squirt.

1499
00:54:23,560 --> 00:54:26,263
-I mixed it with perilla oil.
-It was perilla oil.

1500
00:54:26,329 --> 00:54:29,232
I know that
this is going to mess with me later,

1501
00:54:29,299 --> 00:54:30,634
but I can't stop eating it.

1502
00:54:30,700 --> 00:54:33,003
-You keep craving it.
-It's not too spicy.

1503
00:54:33,069 --> 00:54:35,405
Next, Chef Eun-yeong's dish.

1504
00:54:36,840 --> 00:54:38,174
YUM

1505
00:54:38,508 --> 00:54:39,442
Thank you.

1506
00:54:41,044 --> 00:54:42,846
-Mmm.
-It's not spicy.

1507
00:54:42,912 --> 00:54:44,381
It's not too spicy.

1508
00:54:44,447 --> 00:54:46,883
-You have a high spice tolerance.
-It's not that.

1509
00:54:46,950 --> 00:54:49,219
You're just not used
to the spiciness of <i>mala.</i>

1510
00:54:49,286 --> 00:54:50,720
The spiciness isn't that bad.

1511
00:54:51,054 --> 00:54:52,555
PICKING ON IT

1512
00:54:52,789 --> 00:54:53,890
DROPPING THE SOUP…

1513
00:54:53,957 --> 00:54:55,959
Eun-yeong's dish looks insanely spicy too.

1514
00:54:56,026 --> 00:54:57,861
Sam's only pretending to try it.

1515
00:54:57,927 --> 00:54:58,995
SURPRISED SAM

1516
00:55:00,130 --> 00:55:01,431
LOL

1517
00:55:01,498 --> 00:55:02,332
Try it already.

1518
00:55:02,399 --> 00:55:04,801
-You can see it from there?
-Yes.

1519
00:55:04,868 --> 00:55:06,169
PRETENDING TO EAT IT

1520
00:55:08,538 --> 00:55:09,873
I had it. It's too spicy.

1521
00:55:09,939 --> 00:55:11,141
Tell us what it was like.

1522
00:55:12,375 --> 00:55:13,410
I don't understand…

1523
00:55:13,476 --> 00:55:14,544
It's incomprehensible to me.

1524
00:55:16,212 --> 00:55:17,580
"Why do people want this?"

1525
00:55:17,647 --> 00:55:20,650
Ho-young's dish made my heart spicy,

1526
00:55:20,717 --> 00:55:22,085
but Eun-yeong's dish fired up my mouth.

1527
00:55:22,152 --> 00:55:24,187
-You're right. Exhilaration.
-Simply put, yes.

1528
00:55:24,821 --> 00:55:28,725
A BITE OF THE PORK SKIN SALAD

1529
00:55:29,592 --> 00:55:30,994
-Mmm.
-This is good.

1530
00:55:31,061 --> 00:55:31,995
This is good.

1531
00:55:32,062 --> 00:55:35,131
-Water parsley salad is great, right?
-It's such a kick.

1532
00:55:35,198 --> 00:55:36,733
When you bite on the pork skin

1533
00:55:36,800 --> 00:55:39,202
along with the water parsley,

1534
00:55:39,269 --> 00:55:41,338
it spreads all over your mouth.

1535
00:55:41,404 --> 00:55:43,073
MOUTH FULL OF WATER PARSLEY AROMA

1536
00:55:43,139 --> 00:55:45,608
-It pops in your mouth with every bite.
-Water parsley.

1537
00:55:45,675 --> 00:55:47,510
"Water parsley. I'm water parsley."

1538
00:55:47,577 --> 00:55:49,312
"I'm water parsley."

1539
00:55:49,379 --> 00:55:52,449
-"I'm water parsley"?
-That happens in your mouth.

1540
00:55:52,515 --> 00:55:54,584
It's now time to choose.

1541
00:55:54,651 --> 00:55:56,686
-It must be hard.
-Pick up the remote control.

1542
00:55:56,753 --> 00:55:59,622
If you pick the same chef, that's it.

1543
00:55:59,689 --> 00:56:03,793
-But if you don't, you'll talk it out.
-It's tough.

1544
00:56:03,860 --> 00:56:05,962
<i>Press one to choose Park Eun-yeong</i>

1545
00:56:06,029 --> 00:56:07,430
<i>and two for Jung Ho-young.</i>

1546
00:56:08,064 --> 00:56:11,534
BIBI and NaKyoung, make your choice
and press the button.

1547
00:56:12,969 --> 00:56:14,738
The two sisters' choice.

1548
00:56:14,804 --> 00:56:16,139
They haven't talked yet.

1549
00:56:16,206 --> 00:56:18,508
-They both picked one.
-They did?

1550
00:56:18,575 --> 00:56:19,809
The verdict is out.

1551
00:56:19,876 --> 00:56:22,379
Their choices were

1552
00:56:23,747 --> 00:56:24,881
different.

1553
00:56:25,648 --> 00:56:26,716
It's a tie.

1554
00:56:27,350 --> 00:56:28,318
Really?

1555
00:56:28,685 --> 00:56:30,453
1:1, A TIE

1556
00:56:30,520 --> 00:56:33,056
Most sisters would choose the same thing.

1557
00:56:33,123 --> 00:56:35,859
-They chose different chefs.
-No way.

1558
00:56:35,925 --> 00:56:38,628
Then we'll give you some time to chat.

1559
00:56:38,695 --> 00:56:40,864
-Come here.
-But talk quietly.

1560
00:56:40,930 --> 00:56:42,265
-Where are we going?
-Where to?

1561
00:56:42,332 --> 00:56:44,601
Don't let BIBI make the decision.

1562
00:56:44,667 --> 00:56:46,603
Have a good discussion.

1563
00:56:46,669 --> 00:56:48,171
"You have to listen to me."

1564
00:56:48,238 --> 00:56:49,739
"Number one is better."

1565
00:56:49,806 --> 00:56:53,643
BIBI always had more power
over the remote control.

1566
00:56:54,210 --> 00:56:56,045
-Okay.
-Good.

1567
00:56:56,112 --> 00:56:58,915
-We're done talking.
-You are? All right.

1568
00:56:58,982 --> 00:57:00,183
This is the final decision.

1569
00:57:00,250 --> 00:57:01,951
Please press the button.

1570
00:57:03,086 --> 00:57:04,988
One for Eun-yeong, two for Ho-young.

1571
00:57:05,054 --> 00:57:06,689
BIBI WILL CHOOSE FOR THE TWO OF THEM

1572
00:57:07,223 --> 00:57:08,658
The result is in.

1573
00:57:09,626 --> 00:57:12,295
Whose opinion had a bigger impact?

1574
00:57:12,362 --> 00:57:14,864
Let's find out their decision.

1575
00:57:14,931 --> 00:57:15,932
Show us!

1576
00:57:16,933 --> 00:57:19,536
Spicy Sisters' Fiery Make-up Food.

1577
00:57:19,602 --> 00:57:21,538
Ho-young's Korean spiciness

1578
00:57:21,604 --> 00:57:23,706
versus Eun-yeong's Chinese spiciness.

1579
00:57:23,773 --> 00:57:27,477
Which side did they choose?

1580
00:57:27,544 --> 00:57:28,745
Is it Ho-young

1581
00:57:29,245 --> 00:57:30,513
or Eun-yeong?

1582
00:57:31,614 --> 00:57:33,683
Who did BIBI and NaKyoung choose?

1583
00:57:36,286 --> 00:57:38,121
Who did BIBI and NaKyoung choose?

1584
00:57:38,188 --> 00:57:39,823
JUNG HO-YOUNG WINS

1585
00:57:39,889 --> 00:57:40,723
It was Ho-young.

1586
00:57:40,957 --> 00:57:43,026
GREEN ONION KIMCHI,
GALBI-JJIM, RED RED WINS

1587
00:57:43,493 --> 00:57:46,863
KOREAN SPICINESS WINS

1588
00:57:47,397 --> 00:57:48,932
I actually thought it would be

1589
00:57:48,998 --> 00:57:51,568
-an easy win for the <i>mala </i>dish.
-Me too.

1590
00:57:51,634 --> 00:57:53,670
The pork skin was so delicious.

1591
00:57:53,736 --> 00:57:58,475
-I first chose Chef Eun-yeong.
-And I, Chef Ho-young.

1592
00:57:58,541 --> 00:58:00,510
<i>But NaKyoung said,</i>

1593
00:58:00,577 --> 00:58:02,312
"Which one would you eat every day?"

1594
00:58:03,646 --> 00:58:05,315
Both dishes were so good.

1595
00:58:05,381 --> 00:58:06,916
They'll have to fight daily.

1596
00:58:06,983 --> 00:58:08,985
1 FIGHT AND 1 FOOD TO MAKE UP EACH DAY

1597
00:58:09,052 --> 00:58:10,820
The fight <i>galbi.</i>

1598
00:58:10,887 --> 00:58:13,389
-It can cause discord within the family.
-Probably.

1599
00:58:14,257 --> 00:58:16,192
Congratulations, Chef Ho-young!

1600
00:58:16,259 --> 00:58:19,162
JUNG HO-YOUNG, GREEN GREEN

1601
00:58:19,229 --> 00:58:20,597
-It was so good.
-He wins

1602
00:58:20,663 --> 00:58:24,133
against Chef Eun-yeong
and takes his ninth star. Congrats!

1603
00:58:24,701 --> 00:58:25,869
Congratulations, Ho-young.

1604
00:58:26,836 --> 00:58:31,574
<i>Let us begin the second cook-off</i>
<i>with BIBI and NaKyoung's ingredients.</i>

1605
00:58:31,641 --> 00:58:33,142
The two good-looking chefs.

1606
00:58:33,209 --> 00:58:36,346
The older brother with a great physique,
Chef Choi Hyun-suk.

1607
00:58:36,412 --> 00:58:38,748
GREAT PHYSIQUE

1608
00:58:38,815 --> 00:58:40,683
The sexy grandpa.

1609
00:58:40,750 --> 00:58:43,486
The younger brother with good looks

1610
00:58:43,553 --> 00:58:45,822
and good vibes, Chef Son Jong-won.

1611
00:58:45,889 --> 00:58:46,723
Jong-won.

1612
00:58:46,789 --> 00:58:49,826
Did you say this order is special to you?

1613
00:58:49,893 --> 00:58:51,160
Is there a reason?

1614
00:58:51,661 --> 00:58:54,531
Gyeongsang-do, especially Ulsan,
has a special place in my heart.

1615
00:58:54,597 --> 00:58:57,500
-Is it another hometown?
-He has too many hometowns.

1616
00:58:57,567 --> 00:59:01,070
One of the biggest reasons
why I got into culinary arts

1617
00:59:01,137 --> 00:59:03,106
was the time
when I went to Ulsan with my dad

1618
00:59:03,172 --> 00:59:05,508
and had fresh <i>hoe</i>, fish fillet,
right from the sea.

1619
00:59:05,575 --> 00:59:10,446
I realized how powerful it was
to eat something in its origin.

1620
00:59:10,513 --> 00:59:13,316
I wanted to start sharing that
with my customers.

1621
00:59:14,050 --> 00:59:16,619
-I had this idea when I was six.
-Six?

1622
00:59:16,686 --> 00:59:19,689
-Age six?
-Kids that young don't eat raw fish.

1623
00:59:19,756 --> 00:59:22,692
-It's like my second hometown as a chef.
-His second hometown.

1624
00:59:22,759 --> 00:59:25,361
-He has too many hometowns.
-Because of <i>hoe?</i>

1625
00:59:25,428 --> 00:59:29,332
-Too many hometowns.
-He's doing that again to win a star.

1626
00:59:29,399 --> 00:59:31,501
Is it because
we're doing a sibling special?

1627
00:59:31,568 --> 00:59:33,803
We received lots of heartwarming stories.

1628
00:59:33,870 --> 00:59:37,440
-What is it?
-Chef Jong-won picked Hyun-suk

1629
00:59:37,941 --> 00:59:40,443
as the chef he wanted
to have as an older brother.

1630
00:59:40,510 --> 00:59:43,079
-As an older brother?
-Is there a special reason?

1631
00:59:43,146 --> 00:59:45,181
I'm an only child,

1632
00:59:45,248 --> 00:59:48,051
so I was always jealous
of friends who had an older brother.

1633
00:59:48,117 --> 00:59:50,253
When I joined the show,
he was considerate like a brother,

1634
00:59:50,320 --> 00:59:51,821
welcomed me warmly,

1635
00:59:51,888 --> 00:59:53,790
complimented me often, and always showed

1636
00:59:53,856 --> 00:59:56,125
the biggest laughter at my lousy jokes.

1637
00:59:57,493 --> 00:59:58,828
He can't hide his smile.

1638
00:59:58,895 --> 01:00:00,930
CAN'T HIDE HIS HAPPINESS

1639
01:00:00,997 --> 01:00:03,566
He's 12 years older than you,
so he's more like an uncle.

1640
01:00:03,633 --> 01:00:05,301
It'd be odd to call him <i>hyeong.</i>

1641
01:00:05,368 --> 01:00:06,869
Can he call you that?

1642
01:00:06,936 --> 01:00:10,106
If someone like Jong-won
would call me <i>hyeong,</i>

1643
01:00:10,173 --> 01:00:12,809
I can give him everything I have,
even my organs.

1644
01:00:14,477 --> 01:00:15,311
Do it and take it all.

1645
01:00:15,378 --> 01:00:17,714
I believe your organs aren't too healthy.

1646
01:00:17,780 --> 01:00:20,416
His gall bladder isn't making bile liquid.

1647
01:00:21,417 --> 01:00:22,385
Jong-won doesn't need it.

1648
01:00:22,452 --> 01:00:24,854
-It's useless.
-It won't be much help.

1649
01:00:24,921 --> 01:00:26,422
I thought he'd give me his

1650
01:00:26,489 --> 01:00:28,324
if I gave him mine. Happy now?

1651
01:00:28,558 --> 01:00:30,360
AS ALWAYS, IT'S FUN TO TEASE HIM

1652
01:00:30,426 --> 01:00:32,095
Well, he wants to call you <i>hyeong,</i>

1653
01:00:32,161 --> 01:00:34,731
so why don't you do that
and say what you want to?

1654
01:00:34,797 --> 01:00:36,666
ALREADY FEELS AWKWARD

1655
01:00:36,733 --> 01:00:37,867
Hyun-suk <i>hyeong.</i>

1656
01:00:38,768 --> 01:00:39,969
Go easy on me.

1657
01:00:40,036 --> 01:00:41,270
SMILING

1658
01:00:41,337 --> 01:00:43,673
-"Go easy on me."
-"Go easy on me."

1659
01:00:43,740 --> 01:00:45,308
Jong-won also said this.

1660
01:00:45,375 --> 01:00:47,944
"Chef Hyun-suk is the pillar of the show."

1661
01:00:48,011 --> 01:00:49,879
You said that?

1662
01:00:49,946 --> 01:00:51,214
Why are you so nice to him?

1663
01:00:51,280 --> 01:00:54,484
No matter what we do to tease him,
he's always a good sport about it.

1664
01:00:54,550 --> 01:00:55,618
That's true.

1665
01:00:55,685 --> 01:00:58,821
But because of his age,
the pillar has been tilted a little.

1666
01:00:58,888 --> 01:01:00,356
Like the Tower at Pisa?

1667
01:01:00,423 --> 01:01:02,659
It's going sideways.

1668
01:01:02,725 --> 01:01:04,494
-But he holds out.
-He does.

1669
01:01:04,560 --> 01:01:06,863
Well, as long as he holds out.

1670
01:01:06,929 --> 01:01:08,831
Thank you for your kind words.

1671
01:01:08,898 --> 01:01:11,868
But this show is as wild as the savanna.

1672
01:01:11,934 --> 01:01:13,302
Whenever I am

1673
01:01:13,369 --> 01:01:15,538
frolicking around like a gazelle,

1674
01:01:15,605 --> 01:01:16,873
Jung-hwan would come

1675
01:01:16,939 --> 01:01:19,909
like a lion and bite my neck.

1676
01:01:19,976 --> 01:01:21,310
So while I'm in pain,

1677
01:01:21,377 --> 01:01:23,980
someone like Ho-young comes along

1678
01:01:24,047 --> 01:01:25,815
and nibbles on my offal.

1679
01:01:27,016 --> 01:01:29,118
-It's the best part.
-It's delicious.

1680
01:01:29,185 --> 01:01:30,453
He's yummy.

1681
01:01:30,520 --> 01:01:32,855
But while you're hunted down,

1682
01:01:32,922 --> 01:01:34,590
the one who shows the biggest laughter

1683
01:01:34,657 --> 01:01:36,759
-is Chef Jong-won.
-Right.

1684
01:01:36,826 --> 01:01:38,961
-He loves it.
-He really does.

1685
01:01:39,028 --> 01:01:42,965
He's like a tourist in a car
on a safari trip.

1686
01:01:43,633 --> 01:01:45,435
"He's getting hunted down."

1687
01:01:46,002 --> 01:01:47,637
"They're eating him so happily."

1688
01:01:47,704 --> 01:01:51,941
Jong-won had been quietly nibbling
on Hyun-suk in the shadows.

1689
01:01:52,008 --> 01:01:53,810
This place is a jungle.

1690
01:01:54,644 --> 01:01:56,713
CHEF HYUN-SUK'S INGREDIENTS:
SHRIMP, CUTLASSFISH, SEA SQUIRT

1691
01:01:56,779 --> 01:01:58,815
FLYING FISH ROE, SCALLOP, OYSTER SAUCE,
WATER PARSLEY, PORK FAT, AND MORE

1692
01:01:58,881 --> 01:02:01,350
What are you making, Chef Hyun-suk?

1693
01:02:01,417 --> 01:02:03,052
The name of my dish is…

1694
01:02:03,119 --> 01:02:05,421
-Is cutlassfish your main ingredient?
-Yes.

1695
01:02:05,488 --> 01:02:07,790
I'll fillet the fish,
grind it with shrimp and pork fat,

1696
01:02:07,857 --> 01:02:10,693
make fish balls, and put them on a skewer.

1697
01:02:10,760 --> 01:02:13,663
And then, I'll make hearty broth
full of seafood

1698
01:02:13,730 --> 01:02:15,898
along with a Chinese-style
glass noodle stir-fry.

1699
01:02:15,965 --> 01:02:17,333
Glass noodle stir-fry?

1700
01:02:17,734 --> 01:02:19,502
HARD TO IMAGINE

1701
01:02:19,569 --> 01:02:21,471
I'm not a Chinese chef,

1702
01:02:21,537 --> 01:02:24,607
so I might need some of your help.

1703
01:02:24,674 --> 01:02:26,075
I'll help you. Don't worry.

1704
01:02:26,142 --> 01:02:27,944
-No, not you.
-I'll help.

1705
01:02:28,010 --> 01:02:29,479
Let me help. Don't worry.

1706
01:02:29,545 --> 01:02:31,180
Nobody help him. I got him.

1707
01:02:32,815 --> 01:02:34,517
Don't say anything. Don't move.

1708
01:02:34,584 --> 01:02:36,986
-What about me?
-No, Eun-yeong. I'll help.

1709
01:02:37,053 --> 01:02:38,554
Don't you dare come near me.

1710
01:02:40,456 --> 01:02:42,492
CHEF JONG-WON'S INGREDIENTS:
SHRIMP, SEA SQUIRT, MELJEOT, SCALLOP

1711
01:02:42,558 --> 01:02:44,627
COCONUT CREAM, WATER PARSLEY,
RICE PAPER, SPICY SNACK, AND MORE

1712
01:02:44,694 --> 01:02:46,662
What are you making, Jong-won?

1713
01:02:46,729 --> 01:02:49,932
I want to portray the spirit
of the Gyeongsang-do sisters

1714
01:02:49,999 --> 01:02:52,969
by using fresh seafood
right from the Ulsan sea.

1715
01:02:53,035 --> 01:02:54,537
It's called

1716
01:02:54,604 --> 01:02:56,839
BIBI, NaKyoung, as Real as Ever.

1717
01:02:56,906 --> 01:02:58,674
-As real as ever.
-As real as ever.

1718
01:02:58,741 --> 01:03:00,576
I kind of like the title.

1719
01:03:00,643 --> 01:03:03,546
-It's Gyeongsang dialect. As real as ever.
-As real as ever.

1720
01:03:03,613 --> 01:03:05,181
Do you still use dialect?

1721
01:03:05,248 --> 01:03:07,049
"It's quite hot."

1722
01:03:07,316 --> 01:03:09,585
HER DIALECT IS AS REAL AS EVER

1723
01:03:09,652 --> 01:03:13,322
I can actually speak like I'm from Seoul.

1724
01:03:13,389 --> 01:03:14,891
But when I'm with her,

1725
01:03:14,957 --> 01:03:16,926
it becomes rather tough to keep up.

1726
01:03:16,993 --> 01:03:18,161
-"Tough to keep up."
-"Tough to keep up."

1727
01:03:18,227 --> 01:03:20,296
-Tough to keep up.
-Tough to keep up.

1728
01:03:20,363 --> 01:03:22,365
WE NOW LIVE IN SEOUL

1729
01:03:22,431 --> 01:03:24,901
I see. What's your dish like?

1730
01:03:24,967 --> 01:03:28,204
They had lots of special ingredients.

1731
01:03:28,271 --> 01:03:31,440
There's a variety of pasta
called <i>conchiglie.</i>

1732
01:03:31,507 --> 01:03:34,110
I want to make them look like escargot

1733
01:03:34,177 --> 01:03:37,313
-and fill them with scallop meat.
-With scallops?

1734
01:03:37,380 --> 01:03:41,350
Next, I'll use shrimp
and make it crispy with some rice paper.

1735
01:03:41,417 --> 01:03:45,321
In the previous round,
you seemed to be craving a drink,

1736
01:03:45,388 --> 01:03:48,157
-so I'll make a James Bond-style drink.
-James Bond?

1737
01:03:48,224 --> 01:03:50,593
-A dirty martini.
-A dirty martini?

1738
01:03:50,660 --> 01:03:53,796
-But with stalked sea squirt.
-Sea squirt dirty martini?

1739
01:03:53,863 --> 01:03:57,633
There's a hint of a cucumber-like smell
in stalked sea squirt, so I'll take that

1740
01:03:57,700 --> 01:04:00,736
and put it inside the heart of palm.

1741
01:04:00,803 --> 01:04:03,673
Then I'll blend soju
and create a perfectly balanced cocktail.

1742
01:04:03,739 --> 01:04:05,908
You're blending soju?

1743
01:04:05,975 --> 01:04:08,077
-They have different soju brands.
-Wait.

1744
01:04:08,144 --> 01:04:10,313
Can he do all that in 15 minutes?

1745
01:04:10,379 --> 01:04:12,148
It may not happen.

1746
01:04:12,215 --> 01:04:14,016
We cut them off right on time.

1747
01:04:14,083 --> 01:04:15,518
Then it'll just be dirty.

1748
01:04:16,586 --> 01:04:18,187
It'll take some points off.

1749
01:04:18,254 --> 01:04:20,556
-Not dirty martini?
-Just dirty.

1750
01:04:20,623 --> 01:04:23,559
Let us begin the actual round.

1751
01:04:23,626 --> 01:04:26,562
Gyeongsang-do Sisters' Soul Food.

1752
01:04:26,629 --> 01:04:27,964
-Wow.
-Wow.

1753
01:04:28,030 --> 01:04:29,932
-That's really cool.
-It's so cool.

1754
01:04:29,999 --> 01:04:33,002
He'll show the years of experience
as someone 12 years older,

1755
01:04:33,069 --> 01:04:34,770
Chef Choi Hyun-suk.

1756
01:04:34,837 --> 01:04:38,507
He'll devour Hyun-suk
until the very last bite,

1757
01:04:38,574 --> 01:04:41,143
Chef Son Jong-won.

1758
01:04:41,210 --> 01:04:43,479
Which one of the chefs will fill

1759
01:04:43,546 --> 01:04:44,981
BIBI and NaKyoung's souls?

1760
01:04:45,047 --> 01:04:48,651
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

1761
01:04:49,652 --> 01:04:51,153
Wow.

1762
01:04:51,220 --> 01:04:53,189
-Gyeongsang-do's soul food.
-Two good chefs.

1763
01:04:53,256 --> 01:04:54,490
Can you peel the shrimp?

1764
01:04:54,557 --> 01:04:55,858
Peel the shrimp for me.

1765
01:04:56,525 --> 01:04:57,860
A DISCREET MOVE

1766
01:04:57,927 --> 01:04:59,695
Seafood is everything.

1767
01:04:59,762 --> 01:05:02,131
-He's prepping shrimp.
-Prepping shrimp.

1768
01:05:02,198 --> 01:05:05,968
Hyun-suk rarely ever makes
anything Chinese-inspired.

1769
01:05:06,035 --> 01:05:07,703
These sisters are from Gyeongsang-do.

1770
01:05:07,770 --> 01:05:08,871
Pasta.

1771
01:05:08,938 --> 01:05:10,573
And shrimp.

1772
01:05:11,207 --> 01:05:13,976
It'll take a while for that pasta to cook.

1773
01:05:14,277 --> 01:05:15,411
PULLS OUT A TIMER

1774
01:05:15,478 --> 01:05:17,513
-Ten minutes until al dente.
-Ten minutes.

1775
01:05:17,580 --> 01:05:19,215
He's boiling the shrimp.

1776
01:05:19,282 --> 01:05:21,017
And straight into cold water.

1777
01:05:21,083 --> 01:05:22,818
It's to peel them off easily.

1778
01:05:22,885 --> 01:05:23,753
To peel them off.

1779
01:05:23,819 --> 01:05:26,856
So it's easier to peel them off
if you put them in cold water?

1780
01:05:27,924 --> 01:05:29,258
Cutlassfish.

1781
01:05:29,325 --> 01:05:31,694
-It's cutlassfish.
-He's cutting it

1782
01:05:31,761 --> 01:05:34,297
to remove just the meat and use that.

1783
01:05:34,363 --> 01:05:35,498
-Wow.
-Wow.

1784
01:05:35,831 --> 01:05:38,000
A NICE, CLEAN CUT

1785
01:05:38,501 --> 01:05:39,869
TA-DA

1786
01:05:39,936 --> 01:05:42,038
-My gosh.
-All right.

1787
01:05:42,104 --> 01:05:44,573
-He's showing what he has done.
-Looking prim.

1788
01:05:44,640 --> 01:05:46,475
He's really good at filleting.

1789
01:05:47,543 --> 01:05:49,378
He's making square shapes.

1790
01:05:49,612 --> 01:05:51,280
FINISHED PREPPING THE SHRIMP

1791
01:05:51,614 --> 01:05:53,049
PORK FAT, OYSTER SAUCE, GROUND PEPPER

1792
01:05:53,115 --> 01:05:54,250
AND FINISHING WITH

1793
01:05:54,317 --> 01:05:55,384
SOME SALT

1794
01:05:55,451 --> 01:05:56,819
-Adding salt.
-It didn't go in.

1795
01:05:56,886 --> 01:05:59,322
-I saw it too. It didn't go in.
-He didn't make it.

1796
01:05:59,388 --> 01:06:00,556
-Not even one grain.
-Nothing?

1797
01:06:00,623 --> 01:06:02,525
Nothing at all.
He can't throw it in anymore.

1798
01:06:02,591 --> 01:06:04,660
Did you see him do this?

1799
01:06:04,727 --> 01:06:06,963
-We got to see it live.
-Right?

1800
01:06:07,029 --> 01:06:08,164
What is he grinding?

1801
01:06:08,230 --> 01:06:11,667
-He's grinding shrimp meat.
-Shrimp meat.

1802
01:06:12,635 --> 01:06:15,404
He's grinding his life.

1803
01:06:15,471 --> 01:06:16,639
THE LID WON'T CLICK

1804
01:06:16,872 --> 01:06:17,807
…CAN'T SEE

1805
01:06:17,873 --> 01:06:20,009
-But he became farsighted.
-The lid isn't in place.

1806
01:06:20,076 --> 01:06:22,278
-And his grip is weak.
-He's grinding the shrimp.

1807
01:06:22,345 --> 01:06:24,280
Two minutes have gone by.

1808
01:06:24,347 --> 01:06:26,115
-Time flies.
-What now?

1809
01:06:26,182 --> 01:06:28,284
Time passes by too quickly.

1810
01:06:28,884 --> 01:06:30,720
GRABS AN ONION

1811
01:06:32,288 --> 01:06:34,457
CHOPPED INSTANTLY

1812
01:06:34,523 --> 01:06:35,992
-What's that? Shrimp?
-Shrimp.

1813
01:06:36,058 --> 01:06:37,927
I'm making tomato sauce right now,

1814
01:06:37,994 --> 01:06:40,796
but it's going to be spicy,
so it's called diavolo sauce.

1815
01:06:40,863 --> 01:06:42,298
-Diavolo.
-Diavolo.

1816
01:06:42,631 --> 01:06:43,833
1. COOK GARLIC, ONION IN OLIVE OIL

1817
01:06:43,899 --> 01:06:45,835
Are you boiling something?

1818
01:06:45,901 --> 01:06:48,404
She said she liked shrimp heads,
so I'll grind those too.

1819
01:06:48,471 --> 01:06:50,806
ADDING SHRIMP HEADS TO THE SAUCE

1820
01:06:50,873 --> 01:06:52,875
That's a touch for BIBI.

1821
01:06:52,942 --> 01:06:54,810
-He'll grind shrimp heads.
-Shrimp heads.

1822
01:06:55,344 --> 01:06:57,446
-Tomato sauce.
-It looks good.

1823
01:06:57,513 --> 01:06:58,447
CHILI FLAKES FOR SPICINESS

1824
01:06:58,514 --> 01:07:01,650
-He's opening the soju bottle.
-The red cap.

1825
01:07:01,717 --> 01:07:05,488
It's usually made with white wine,
but he's using soju instead.

1826
01:07:07,156 --> 01:07:08,591
What are those? Chili peppers?

1827
01:07:08,657 --> 01:07:09,959
Snacks.

1828
01:07:10,026 --> 01:07:12,895
-It's the spicy snacks.
-Spicy snacks.

1829
01:07:12,962 --> 01:07:13,896
RED FOR THE SISTERS

1830
01:07:13,963 --> 01:07:14,964
Hot snacks.

1831
01:07:15,297 --> 01:07:16,899
NICELY GROUND SHRIMP MEAT

1832
01:07:16,966 --> 01:07:19,535
He's topping cutlassfish pieces
with shrimp meat.

1833
01:07:19,602 --> 01:07:21,337
-On top?
-Whoa!

1834
01:07:22,138 --> 01:07:25,041
-Cutlassfish <i>menbosha?</i>
-Cutlassfish<i> menbosha?</i>

1835
01:07:25,107 --> 01:07:26,642
-Cutlassfish <i>menbosha?</i>
-I've never heard of it.

1836
01:07:26,709 --> 01:07:28,844
Shrimp and cutlassfish <i>menbosha.</i>

1837
01:07:28,911 --> 01:07:31,714
SC <i>menbosha. </i>It's a new attempt.

1838
01:07:31,781 --> 01:07:33,883
It'll have a nice texture and flavor.

1839
01:07:33,949 --> 01:07:35,851
After all, <i>menbosha </i>is a Chinese dish.

1840
01:07:35,918 --> 01:07:37,520
Would that not fall apart?

1841
01:07:37,586 --> 01:07:40,990
-It'll depend on the batter.
-Yes, he'll have to coat it.

1842
01:07:41,057 --> 01:07:42,691
How much water do I need, Eun-yeong?

1843
01:07:43,259 --> 01:07:44,360
Water? Stop.

1844
01:07:44,427 --> 01:07:45,628
Use your hands.

1845
01:07:45,694 --> 01:07:48,030
-Mix it with your hands.
-No more water.

1846
01:07:48,097 --> 01:07:50,900
-Why did you add more? That's too much!
-That's too much water.

1847
01:07:50,966 --> 01:07:52,568
Pour some out.

1848
01:07:52,635 --> 01:07:55,838
Keep stirring with your hands
until the batter gets thicker.

1849
01:07:55,905 --> 01:07:59,775
He has never made frying batter
in the Chinese way.

1850
01:07:59,842 --> 01:08:00,910
Is that okay?

1851
01:08:00,976 --> 01:08:03,212
-It's a bit thin.
-It looks fine.

1852
01:08:03,279 --> 01:08:05,181
-No, he'll cook it right away.
-Whatever. I'm done.

1853
01:08:05,247 --> 01:08:07,216
-What?
-It does look a bit thin.

1854
01:08:07,283 --> 01:08:09,752
-He put powder on it, so it's fine.
-Will it be okay?

1855
01:08:09,819 --> 01:08:12,488
But you have to cook <i>menbosha</i>
over low heat.

1856
01:08:12,555 --> 01:08:14,290
He can't stick to the original recipe.

1857
01:08:14,356 --> 01:08:15,724
He's not working with bread.

1858
01:08:15,791 --> 01:08:18,527
He's not using bread, so it's okay.

1859
01:08:18,594 --> 01:08:19,862
Four minutes have gone by.

1860
01:08:19,929 --> 01:08:20,763
NEW SEAFOOD: SCALLOP

1861
01:08:20,996 --> 01:08:22,598
INTO DIAVOLO PASTA SAUCE

1862
01:08:22,832 --> 01:08:23,933
GAS LIGHTER

1863
01:08:23,999 --> 01:08:25,401
Can you get my stove?

1864
01:08:26,302 --> 01:08:27,903
Sorry. Thank you.

1865
01:08:27,970 --> 01:08:30,673
-He had a moment to light his stove.
-How nice.

1866
01:08:30,739 --> 01:08:33,175
-How sweet.
-That's what Jong-won does.

1867
01:08:33,609 --> 01:08:34,844
PREPPED SCALLOPS INTO A PAN

1868
01:08:34,910 --> 01:08:35,978
Scallops.

1869
01:08:36,045 --> 01:08:37,079
Your favorite.

1870
01:08:37,146 --> 01:08:38,948
I really love scallops.

1871
01:08:39,014 --> 01:08:40,716
-Scallops?
-I love them.

1872
01:08:41,150 --> 01:08:43,786
2. COOK SCALLOPS IN OLIVE OIL,
THEN BASTE WITH BUTTER

1873
01:08:43,853 --> 01:08:46,689
Scallops become so delicious
when you cook them in a cast iron pan.

1874
01:08:46,755 --> 01:08:48,023
It comes out golden brown.

1875
01:08:48,090 --> 01:08:51,494
-They almost get stuck on the pan.
-Yes.

1876
01:08:51,560 --> 01:08:54,196
I'm making sauce for the fried shrimp

1877
01:08:54,263 --> 01:08:56,465
with coconut and <i>meljeot.</i>

1878
01:08:56,532 --> 01:08:58,601
<i>-Meljeot?</i>
-He's using anchovy <i>jeotgal.</i>

1879
01:08:58,667 --> 01:09:00,603
<i>Meljeot, </i>something the sisters love.

1880
01:09:00,669 --> 01:09:01,704
LOTS OF SEAFOOD EVERYWHERE

1881
01:09:02,371 --> 01:09:04,840
It's hard to imagine
what that dish will be like.

1882
01:09:04,907 --> 01:09:08,244
You won't be able to tell
if you're in Ulsan or Southeast Asia.

1883
01:09:08,777 --> 01:09:10,145
That's a new mix.

1884
01:09:10,212 --> 01:09:12,982
He's trying to create
an exotic taste from the sea.

1885
01:09:13,048 --> 01:09:14,650
His hands, <i>son</i>, move quickly.

1886
01:09:14,717 --> 01:09:16,185
That's why he's Son Jong-won.

1887
01:09:16,252 --> 01:09:17,820
He's peeling the shrimp.

1888
01:09:17,887 --> 01:09:20,890
I guess the 12-year difference
shows in their speed.

1889
01:09:20,956 --> 01:09:22,958
Jong-won makes his sauce.

1890
01:09:24,126 --> 01:09:27,029
HYUN-SUK MAKES HIS SAUCE TOO

1891
01:09:27,096 --> 01:09:31,000
He's crushing shrimp heads
and frying them in a pan.

1892
01:09:31,066 --> 01:09:34,003
BIBI said she liked them,
so he didn't throw them out.

1893
01:09:34,069 --> 01:09:35,437
Kelp seasoning.

1894
01:09:35,504 --> 01:09:36,338
Salted kelps.

1895
01:09:36,405 --> 01:09:37,773
-What's that?
<i>-Mala xiang guo </i>sauce.

1896
01:09:37,840 --> 01:09:38,807
SPLATTER

1897
01:09:39,175 --> 01:09:40,342
MALA SAUCE LAUNCHES

1898
01:09:40,409 --> 01:09:41,577
Oh, no!

1899
01:09:41,644 --> 01:09:43,712
-He's bleeding.
-Quiet.

1900
01:09:43,779 --> 01:09:45,114
Be quiet.

1901
01:09:45,181 --> 01:09:47,550
-What?
-He splattered it all over himself.

1902
01:09:47,616 --> 01:09:49,585
-What? On your uniform?
-On his uniform!

1903
01:09:49,818 --> 01:09:50,653
MALA XIANG GUO SAUCE

1904
01:09:51,887 --> 01:09:53,255
He wouldn't have done that before.

1905
01:09:53,322 --> 01:09:55,391
-No.
-But he's splattering himself.

1906
01:09:55,457 --> 01:09:57,459
He splashed the sauce on his uniform.

1907
01:09:57,526 --> 01:10:00,262
-He's getting older. Oh, no.
-He doesn't have enough power.

1908
01:10:00,329 --> 01:10:03,399
You should wear an apron
over your uniform.

1909
01:10:03,465 --> 01:10:05,734
He keeps it around his waist,
trying to look cool.

1910
01:10:05,968 --> 01:10:07,536
HIS LAST PIECE OF PRIDE

1911
01:10:07,603 --> 01:10:10,573
-I feel bad for him.
-He's getting old.

1912
01:10:10,639 --> 01:10:13,108
-Only eight minutes left?
-Whoa.

1913
01:10:13,175 --> 01:10:15,811
-You've used up seven minutes.
-Time is running out.

1914
01:10:16,212 --> 01:10:17,680
TAKES THE DIAVOLO SAUCE

1915
01:10:17,746 --> 01:10:19,648
Whoa. He's blending that.

1916
01:10:22,985 --> 01:10:23,819
PING!

1917
01:10:24,386 --> 01:10:25,588
EXPLODES

1918
01:10:26,522 --> 01:10:28,257
ANOTHER EMERGENCY SITUATION

1919
01:10:28,457 --> 01:10:29,758
EXPLODING WITH RED FLAVOR

1920
01:10:29,959 --> 01:10:30,926
AN EMERGENCY ALERT

1921
01:10:30,993 --> 01:10:32,127
Was the lid not on securely?

1922
01:10:32,194 --> 01:10:34,663
The blender explodes
when you blend something hot.

1923
01:10:35,764 --> 01:10:38,601
He was in a rush,
so he had no other choice.

1924
01:10:38,667 --> 01:10:41,937
-He's so calm. I'd make a huge fuss.
-He is.

1925
01:10:42,004 --> 01:10:44,106
But he didn't splash it on his uniform.

1926
01:10:44,173 --> 01:10:46,475
-It's only on the rag.
-Yes, he's still young.

1927
01:10:46,542 --> 01:10:47,943
He dodged it right away.

1928
01:10:48,010 --> 01:10:50,379
<i>He pulled back instantly.</i>

1929
01:10:50,446 --> 01:10:52,014
But Hyun-suk couldn't.

1930
01:10:52,081 --> 01:10:53,415
He just stood there and took it.

1931
01:10:53,882 --> 01:10:56,018
PULLING OUT THE PASTA

1932
01:10:57,253 --> 01:10:59,588
3. MIX PASTA WITH DIAVOLO SAUCE
AND MAKE CONCHIGLIE PASTA

1933
01:10:59,655 --> 01:11:00,856
What did he use for that?

1934
01:11:00,923 --> 01:11:04,460
-Shrimp heads, tomato, and pasta?
-Shrimp heads, tomato, and pasta.

1935
01:11:04,693 --> 01:11:05,894
HYUN-SUK USES SCALLOPS TOO

1936
01:11:05,961 --> 01:11:07,429
FEAT. MINCED GARLIC

1937
01:11:07,663 --> 01:11:09,398
GRABS THE MALA SOUP TOO

1938
01:11:09,465 --> 01:11:11,100
He's grinding the shrimp.

1939
01:11:11,166 --> 01:11:13,202
It's because BIBI likes the heads.

1940
01:11:13,269 --> 01:11:15,938
-It's BIBI's favorite.
-That's where all the flavor is.

1941
01:11:16,005 --> 01:11:17,973
Shrimp heads add so much umami.

1942
01:11:18,040 --> 01:11:20,142
You're right.

1943
01:11:20,209 --> 01:11:22,177
The sauce is ready for Hyun-suk.

1944
01:11:22,244 --> 01:11:23,545
That looks good.

1945
01:11:23,612 --> 01:11:25,581
